{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city","id":1534152,"title":"Clan Demographics in your city?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-04-13T13:30:08-07:00","id":1534152,"title":"Clan Demographics in your city?","content":"Just a fun question that I was thinking of.<br /> <br /> Which clan in your game is the most numerous? In our game it's the Brujah, Gangrel, Nosferatu and some Caitiffs and Thin Bloods<br /> <br /> Which Clan is the smallest in your game? For ours it's the Ventrue, Toreador and, Malkavians that has the smallest numbers with 1 or 2 members at most","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T14:44:34-07:00","id":1534159,"title":"","content":"For many years I ran cities as multi-clan just like how the books suggested. But over the last 5-10 years I have been leaning more towards the notion of mono-clan cities with unique laws and cultures. The reason for this is that most of the glass ceiling issues that are prepacked into vampire go away when you make this change and most of the context for problems changes.<br /> <br /> For instance, in a normal multi-clan city you bounce off all the power blocks, the old vs. the new, this clan vs. that clan, this path of enlightenment vs. that path of enlightenment. That is a lot of stuff to navigate before you can deal with surviving lupine or sabbat incursions, or just getting your nightly meal.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, a coterie of a single clan type move to a small town or city without other vampires, they declare themselves prince and primogen of the city, they embrace as they see fit, they enforce laws as they see fit, and they adjust the human society as they see fit without ever needing to come out of the shadows. If they wish to allow other clans or bloodlines to setup shop, then they are constrained to a specific section of town to setup their haven with explicit understanding that they are a long term guest, rather than an equal owner.<br /> <br /> Most multi-clan cities feel cookie cutter to me at this point. Clan A is on top, clan B is supporting them in theory, while clan C is outright trying to upend the power structure. It is pretty much paint by numbers, with the only variables being which clan is in which position.<br /> <br /> Conversely lets look at some of the mono-clan cities I have used.<br /> <br /> Ventrue City:<br /> There is a central blood bank which acts as a blood clearing house for ventrue with specific tastes. You can either get blood packs or get the identity information of the donor that matches your needs so you can fetch them for yourself. All the excess blood isn't ventrue fodder is sold on the world market to hospitals and research facilities. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jOXCrwX1QZ8\" target=\"_blank\">Into the Shadows</a> did a good overview of the blood industry which makes for some interesting vampire ideas. The benefits of this city design is that hunting is practically never done, so most of the headlines or activities that hunters use to detect a vampire presence never happen.<br /> <br /> Nagaraja City: <br /> Between the need for meat and the obvious teeth modifications, Nagaraja have a hard time blending in. So I based a mono-bloodline city around the concept of <a href=\"https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0805570/?ref_=nv_sr_srsg_0_tt_8_nm_0_q_midnight%2520meat\" target=\"_blank\">Midnight Meat Train</a>. A single ghoul hunts people who won't be missed or who prey upon the community. This once again keeps the public face of the city very clean and orderly. Because you can only fight necromancy with necromancy most other supernaturals avoid the city. Furthermore if there are lean times, the Nagaraja can eat the ghosts of their victims for a second meal. This creates a feeling of a group meal and almost a holy ritual being carried out, rather than each vampire doing their own thing.<br /> <br /> Gangrel City:<br /> I used this in a historical game, where the pueblo cities in america were actually society experiments set up by gangrels. The people were cut off from the ground below while the vampires ran the cities during the night. When the vampire lords were defeated the mortals finally abandoned the cities. All in all it was a symbiotic relationship, the vampires hunted and provided food for the mortals as well as security, while the mortals looked after them while they slept during the day, at least until they could earth meld.<br /> <br /> Very different cities, very different cultures, and very different views on mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4},{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T15:36:01-07:00","id":1534164,"title":"","content":"That's an interesting idea Thoth about single clan dominant cities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T17:05:20-07:00","id":1534171,"title":"","content":"Look... this is more of my personal impression than anything, but generalizing it would be something like this:<br /> <br /> The most numerous would be the Brujah, Nosferatu, Malkavian, Toreador and the Followers of Set, the first two being much more than the others due to the impetuous nature of many Brujah and the criteria and discretion of the Nosferatu.<br /> <br /> More in the middle would have the Caitff, Gangrel, Dominated Malkavians, Assamites, Tzimice and the Lasombra. The latter would have less than the others, due to its high selection criteria.<br /> <br /> Those with fewer members would be: Ventrue, Tremere, Ravnos, Giovanni, Samedi, Banu Haqim, Saulot Antitribu, True Brujah, Daughters of Cacaphony, Nagaraja and finally Saulot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T08:38:37-07:00","id":1534196,"title":"","content":"The idea of mono-clan cities is certainly very appropriete for smaller towns while also offering an alternative to dive deeper into a specific clan rather than get all or many clans with a much more shallow treatment.<br /> <br /> I like your idea, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a> . I like it alot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T11:01:18-07:00","id":1534201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534171#post1534171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The most numerous would be the Brujah, Nosferatu, Malkavian, Toreador and the Followers of Set, the first two being much more than the others due to the impetuous nature of many Brujah and the criteria and discretion of the Nosferatu.</div> </div> </div> </div>Whenever I have tried to work out the population ratios, I keep running into the issue that while a Clan like the Brujah might have one of the highest embrace rates since they are guided by their passions, they also will have one of the highest attrition rates due to them being hot headed and doing stupid stuff. The same goes for the Toreador in that they will embrace on a whim, but the artistic types are not really known for their emotional balance or fortitude and thus have a higher instance of self-destruction.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel present an interesting issue in this regard, they want survivors. Thus they are generally self limiting when selecting a potential embrace, which means even if they wanted to embrace more, the worthwhile candidates are few and far between. But once they get past the first couple of years and get their disciplines up and running, they have a higher likelihood of surviving as a vampire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T11:51:01-07:00","id":1534205,"title":"","content":"Individual cities could have any kind of clan demographics because so much depends on the individual vampires and the history of that domain. Large cities tend to have more diversity in clans, but small cities (metro areas under 500k) tend to be made up of only one clan (the Prince and her brood). <br /> <br /> However, I do have general world population of the clans in my head. Any percentages are only top of my head figures, not firm numbers. Below is a rough estimate based on the original setting of the game, as I ignore almost all metaplot. Therefore, some of my statements will not make sense to those who accept all the changes or assumptions in Revised and V5.<br /> <br /> The Brujah are the most numerous vampires because they are numerous in all sects and their nature makes them prone to a lot of rash embraces. They are well over half the Anarchs, one of the larger clans in the Camarilla (though in many domains there is not much distinction between an &quot;anarch&quot; and other vampires who accept the Camarilla other than the &quot;anarch&quot; is currently out with the local Prince. The moment local politics changes that vampire is no longer an &quot;anarch&quot;.), and the Brujah anti-tribu are almost as numerous in the Sabbat as the Lasombra and Tzimisce. If you combine the Cam/Anarch into one, the Brujah probably make up around 25% of all vampires. In the Sabbat, they are only 15% or so.<br /> <br /> The Toreador are probably the next numerous clan. It's probably the second largest clan in the Camarilla, and it still has respectable numbers in the Sabbat though behind the big three. They probably comprise around 20% of the Camarilla and 10% of the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> The Ventrue are probably next. They are another major clan of the Camarilla, often establish successful broods, and are the largest clan in many domains or countries Having many members being Princes result in siring privileges. But they are very few of them among the Anarchs, and the Ventrue anti-tribu are one of the smaller clans in the Sabbat. Both the Brujah anti-tribu and Ventrue anti-tribu are leaders of the Loyalist movement. They comprise around 15% of the Camarilla, but only 5% or so of the Sabbat (which is a much smaller sect, only a third of the Camarilla).<br /> <br /> Gangrel are next, although much of their population is &quot;hidden&quot; in the sense they reside in small towns and the wilderness as solitary vampires or in small broods rather than easily documented in cities governed by Princes or Bishops. There may not actually be more Camarilla Gangrel than there are, say, Camarilla Nosferatu, but there are a lot of Gangrel who are essentially Autarkis (except when they visit cities and abide by any Camarilla rules there) or independent in the wilderness, and the Sabbat Gangrel (Country and City plus offshoots like the Ahrimanes) collectively make up a significant part of the sect. They are probably around 10% in each sect plus a large number effectively independent. Many of these independents might be loosely affiliated with the Camarilla, Sabbat, or Anarchs, but merely for convenience.<br /> <br /> Nosferatu are probably just behind the Gangrel, and like them are probably around 10% of each sect (meaning the Camarilla Nosferatu are around three times their Sabbat counterparts). The Sabbat Nosferatu usually pack/coven among themselves because they have more in common with their Camarilla counterparts than they do with other Sabbat. The Nosferatu are mainly concerned with survival, and their membership in both sects is more like hedging their bets than anything else. This does not mean there are not differences between them or that there are not conflicts, but they continue to share information and secrets. Of course individual Nosferatu always have their own opinions, so sometimes this cooperation is not mentioned to those individuals who are more vocal about their sect membership and are involved politically with it.<br /> <br /> Malkavians would be next, and are probably under 10% but over 5% of each sect. They are a respectably sized clan, but nothing special. While there are more Camarilla Malkavians, as a percentage of their sect, the Sabbat Malkavians are probably higher because I see 1) the role of Vasantasena in creating/saving the sect after the Convention of Thorns gives the Malkavian anti-tribu a prestige their Camarilla counterparts do not have, 2) their mental problems are probably not considered to be as much of a problem within the Sabbat, and 3) siring is not as controlled in the Sabbat as it is in the Camarilla.<br /> <br /> Lasombra are next. While they are a major component of the Sabbat, maybe 20% or more of the sect, the Sabbat is smaller than the Camarilla and there are very few Lasombra anti-tribu. The Lasombra clan was gutted during the Anarch Revolt with many of their vampires dying during the Anarch Revolt, and their Sabbat numbers collapsed when that sect was devastated by the Camarilla-Sabbat Wars and the first two Sabbat Civil Wars. While their numbers have recovered, it is mainly a result of new neonates being embraced. Being limited to the minority sect means your overall numbers just do not reach those of the Camarilla. The Lasombra probably number less than a third of the Ventrue's total numbers.<br /> <br /> Tzimisce are right under the Lasombra and their numbers are similar. Major components of the Sabbat, but almost no numbers outside of that and suffering the same problems of the clan being gutted during the Anarch Revolt and suffering hard when the Sabbat falls on hard times.<br /> <br /> Tremere are next. Almost all their members are in the Camarilla with only a small number of &quot;House Goratrix&quot; being in the Sabbat. However, even if they are only 5-8% of the Camarilla, since the Camarilla is the largest sect this means they have more numbers than the so-called Independent Clans. In terms of influence and power, they punch above their numbers in the Camarilla. House Goratrix survives because the Lasombra find them useful as allies against the Tzimisce. There are only a handful of chantries, and most Tremere anti-tribu form chantry packs. The Sabbat Tzimisce would eliminate the Tremere anti-tribu if they could so House Goratrix avoids areas where Tzimisce power is high and Lasombra weak.<br /> <br /> Caitiff are likely next, although their numbers may be higher than I initially figure. In some books, the Caitiff seem to be significant, and in other books their numbers are much lower. This probably has more to do with writers being interested in exploring Caitiff rather than a commentary on their true numbers. Over time this population will likely increase in size. I can see an argument that would present them larger than the total number of Tremere. This includes all types - Caitiff, Panders, Thin-Bloods.<br /> <br /> Setites are the most populated independent clan. There are several reasons why their numbers are so low. First, they do not hold power in many domains, and therefore lack opportunities to sire in both of the large sects. Their strongholds are few compared to other clans. Second, they do not get along well with one another. Setite priests operate alone with perhaps a childe. They do not sire large broods, but seek to spread new temples far away from each other. When they don't, infighting quells their numbers. Third, they have lots of enemies who wish them dead - many members of the Camarilla, the Sabbat, Lupines, Hunters, and many others. Their numbers are probably expanding a lot in Africa whose population has increased greatly in the past century, with large growing cities, and in an area where the Camarilla's institutions are weak and the Sabbat's presence is negligible. However, Setite clan culture often leads to feuds and infighting due to competition slows down such growth. <br /> <br /> Ravnos are next simply because both the Assamites and Giovanni have limitations put on them by the Treaty of Tyre and Promise of 1528. In my chronicles, the Camarilla is indeed a worldwide organization so Indian vampires (like Unmada or Bindusara) have always been a part of it from the beginning. India does have more Ravnos than normal, but the traditional clans have always been well represented. A clan of nomads who often irritate the powers that be simply cannot have any substantial numbers. But because they wander widely they are present in many places. They are not as well populated as the Gangrel because they lack the means Gangrel have to hide from Lupines, have enemies in the Gangrel who tend to be better fighters, and lack the political support the Gangrel have in their sect memberships.<br /> <br /> Assamites are next. When your clan organization is a cult of assassins devoted to an esoteric understanding of vampire history and eschatology, your numbers are low. They have a few strongholds outside Alamut ruled by the Old Man of the Mountain, but not many. Where individual vampire assassins are allowed to stay in a domain, like Badr in Milwaukee, they cover a lot of territory because there isn't that much of a need for vampire assassins at any one time or place. To stay busy enough to earn their vitae, they need to cover a lot of ground. There might be just as many Assamite anti-tribu as there are Assamites. They are just limited in numbers because most of them are members of the Black Hand or prospective members.<br /> <br /> Giovanni are the smallest clan. Per original canon, they are only allowed three embraces every generation which is about one every decade. So a total of 50 new Giovanni have been embraced since the Promise of 1528. There are probably a few dozen Giovanni from earlier, but that is it. Even if we combine these numbers with a hundred or so surviving Cappadocians in whatever form (Samedi, Lamia, Harbingers, etc.) there's just not much left of the old Cappadocians. The Anarch Revolt and Giovanni purge hit them hard.<br /> <br /> All the bloodlines are extremely small, perhaps each one being only a dozen or a few dozen at most.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T17:35:12-07:00","id":1534217,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534201#post1534201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Whenever I have tried to work out the population ratios, I keep running into the issue that while a Clan like the Brujah might have one of the highest embrace rates since they are guided by their passions, they also will have one of the highest attrition rates due to them being hot headed and doing stupid stuff. The same goes for the Toreador in that they will embrace on a whim, but the artistic types are not really known for their emotional balance or fortitude and thus have a higher instance of self-destruction.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel present an interesting issue in this regard, they want survivors. Thus they are generally self limiting when selecting a potential embrace, which means even if they wanted to embrace more, the worthwhile candidates are few and far between. But once they get past the first couple of years and get their disciplines up and running, they have a higher likelihood of surviving as a vampire.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if this would be useful, but in my case what happened was that some clans had members, which I &quot;affectionately&quot; called, cannon fodder or disposable members, if you prefer. It works like this, some clans embraced in relatively larger quantities in order to have strength in numbers against external or internal threats, these members often did tasks that were thankless or that others just didn't want to do and many of them weren't even &quot;legally&quot; embraced depending on the clan, they were kept hidden and were only revealed if necessary or if they managed to perform an important task. More specifically.<br /> <br /> The Brujah clan operated in a way similar to street gangs most of the time, as well as being the main source of members, and their cannon fodders were often used as a strike force or even messengers, if one was destroyed, a gang member was promoted to kindred and that was basically all.<br /> <br /> The Nosferatu embraced homeless people and others that no one would want to use as cannon fodder, they also embraced Toreador ghouls and blood bags, like a pet peeve. When it was illegal they only hid in the sewers until needed, I don't know how they fed, but I assume it was rats.<br /> <br /> The Toreadors had cannon fodder who used weapons and even knew martial arts and that's all I can say about then, because otherwise they just existed.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel also had cannon fodder kindreds, but they preferred to use animals and otherwise they only existed too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T21:18:19-07:00","id":1534224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534217#post1534217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know if this would be useful, but in my case what happened was that some clans had members, which I &quot;affectionately&quot; called, cannon fodder or disposable members, if you prefer. It works like this, some clans embraced in relatively larger quantities in order to have strength in numbers against external or internal threats, these members often did tasks that were thankless or that others just didn't want to do and many of them weren't even &quot;legally&quot; embraced depending on the clan, they were kept hidden and were only revealed if necessary or if they managed to perform an important task.</div> </div> </div> </div>The tricky part about this sort of population explanation is that several other parts of the setting start to fall apart. One of the defining differences between the Sabbat and the Camarilla is that only the former does mass embraces, while most of the clans have criteria for embraces.<br /> <br /> Ventrue, Lasombra, and Giovanni want competence above all else.<br /> Tremere and Giovanni are after occult individuals.<br /> Gangrel want survivors.<br /> Toreador will embrace on a whim of emotion, the rest of the time they are trying to preserve an artist for all time, in either case neither are seen as disposable.<br /> Assamites desire combat capable mortals.<br /> The Followers of Set have the morality for mass embrace cannon fodder, but they are very focused on their temple indoctrination, which precludes the strategy.<br /> <br /> So really the only clans that have both the means and morality to go the cannon fodder route are the Brujah and the Nosferatu. The Brujah will throw themselves at the enemy or at an point of contention with wild abandon. But given the passionate behavior of the brujah they are just as likely to view mass embraces as morally repugnant as see it as a viable option.<br /> <br /> The nosferatu on the other hand have options, when the entire nos population of a city can disappear or reappear somewhere else, you don't need to fight head on thus there isn't a real need for mass embrace cannon fodder. This strategy is made even less likely when you consider the nos spawning pools which provide them with hordes of mutant ghoul animals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T03:06:21-07:00","id":1534237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534205#post1534205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Caitiff are likely next, although their numbers may be higher than I initially figure. In some books, the Caitiff seem to be significant, and in other books their numbers are much lower. This probably has more to do with writers being interested in exploring Caitiff rather than a commentary on their true numbers. Over time this population will likely increase in size. I can see an argument that would present them larger than the total number of Tremere. This includes all types - Caitiff, Panders, Thin-Bloods</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Caitiffs should be pretty rare.<br /> That a &quot;clan vampire&quot; sires a non thin blood Caitiff should be pretty rare, Thin-Bloods are likely never sired with permission and non thin blood Caitiffs will at least in the Camarialla also hardly ever get the permission to sire. Meaning that even if the Camarilla doesn't hut down Caitiffs for being Caitiffs, a lot of them get likely still hunted for being sired without permission.<br /> <br /> And Caitiffs were hunted untill pretty recently and now still often used as expendable fodder by the Sects.<br /> <br /> And being high gen without the protection of a clan will also not often lead to a long unlife.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T11:20:12-07:00","id":1534258,"title":"","content":"That would interesting to have a mostly a Brujah, Gangrel, Nosferatu dominated domian and for whatever reasons the Ventrue, Toreador, Tremere or even the Lasombra can't get a foothold at all. But you are seeing The Ministry and Tzmisce showing up and having success.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T14:52:32-07:00","id":1534269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534237#post1534237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Caitiffs should be pretty rare.<br /> That a &quot;clan vampire&quot; sires a non thin blood Caitiff should be pretty rare, Thin-Bloods are likely never sired with permission and non thin blood Caitiffs will at least in the Camarialla also hardly ever get the permission to sire. Meaning that even if the Camarilla doesn't hut down Caitiffs for being Caitiffs, a lot of them get likely still hunted for being sired without permission.<br /> <br /> And Caitiffs were hunted untill pretty recently and now still often used as expendable fodder by the Sects.<br /> <br /> And being high gen without the protection of a clan will also not often lead to a long unlife.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think you are necessarily wrong because you make good points, but it all depends on the assumptions of the ST when designing their chronicle setting.<br /> <br /> In terms of the Sabbat, we know the Panders have existed since the 1950s and their numbers were sufficient to help end the Third Sabbat Civil War fairly quickly. We also know the Sabbat creation rites seem to create a lot more Caitiff than normal. We don't know how big the Panders are, but there are probably a lot more of them than Serpents of the Light, Ravnos anti-tribu, Ahrimanes, and even the Ventrue anti-tribu. I'd probably put their numbers in the middle of the pack along with the Gangrel, Nosferatu, and Malkavian anti-tribu, but noticeably behind the Lasombra, Tzimisce, and Brujah anti-tribu. Of course that is just for that sect. What about everywhere else?<br /> <br /> We know that all clans are pumping out Caitiff at some point. It doesn't seem to me that non-thinblood Caitiff are rare. Nothing stops a PC from deciding to be an 8th generation Caitiff so it is at least theoretically possible and we have various examples of such. In Chicago By Night we know the 9th generation Brujah Dooler created Gordon Keaton. Dickie's Fulcher 8th generation sire, Valerie, appears to have Been Caitiff though we don't know if that was due to diablerie. 7th generation Tremere Abraham DuSable created Maldavis. 8th generation Brujah Sheriff created Neon. CbN originally showed 7 canonical Caitiff plus two more only recently destroyed (though Gengis was mistakenly included in the Brujah section, the Caitiff lineage chart clearly places him there and the Brujah chart does not include him or his sire). 7 is just as many as the Malkavians and Nosferatu, more than the Gangrel if you don't count the Wolfpack, and more than the Tremere. While STs that develop the city canonically can still add a lot more vampires of true clans, to me that indicates there can be a large population of Caitiff compared to the clans. Is Chicago normal or an outlier?<br /> <br /> While Milwaukee By Night only has one named Caitiff character (Turk), it also states &quot;Milwaukee has proven a godsend for the Caitiff&quot; and said the Anubi and Anarch gangs always have at least one Caitiff and that some Caitiff have even gained status in the Camarilla. The ST would need to create these Caitiff of course. Whatever their exact numbers, there are clearly enough of them to be noticeable. Los Angeles by Night only describes three Caitiff, but that has very few characters in it. Gangrels aren't even mentioned, and the Malkavians and Nosferatu get only one character each. But we do know that one of the Barons is Caitiff, promiscuous embracing happens a lot, and lots of Caitiff have fled there.<br /> <br /> But Dark Colony doesn't mention any. New Orleans By Night only mentions one and explicitly says while many Caitiff come to New Orleans few stay and most quickly leave because of persecution. Berlin By Night mentions none and explains that admitting you're Caitiff gets you quickly killed. Caitiff are also not mentioned in DC By Night.<br /> <br /> So how prominent and numerous the Caitiff are appears to be based on how effectively the Prince and elders are able to govern. Where they are powerful, Caitiff are rare. But when the domain is divided politically for a long time, under constant attack, or has a strong Anarch presence, the numbers of Caitiff seem to increase rapidly. It's like any other design element for STs to consider when creating their chronicle.<br /> <br /> Most domains will likely have more Caitiff than they do of any of the old four independent clans. I'll admit that many players now see the old four independent clans as being just as numerous as the original Camarilla 7 and much larger than they were originally described or implied to be, and as such those players will put their numbers much greater than I do.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T22:39:31-07:00","id":1534289,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534269#post1534269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We know that all clans are pumping out Caitiff at some point. It doesn't seem to me that non-thinblood Caitiff are rare. Nothing stops a PC from deciding to be an 8th generation Caitiff so it is at least theoretically possible and we have various examples of such. In Chicago By Night we know the 9th generation Brujah Dooler created Gordon Keaton. Dickie's Fulcher 8th generation sire, Valerie, appears to have Been Caitiff though we don't know if that was due to diablerie. 7th generation Tremere Abraham DuSable created Maldavis. 8th generation Brujah Sheriff created Neon. CbN originally showed 7 canonical Caitiff plus two more only recently destroyed (though Gengis was mistakenly included in the Brujah section, the Caitiff lineage chart clearly places him there and the Brujah chart does not include him or his sire). 7 is just as many as the Malkavians and Nosferatu, more than the Gangrel if you don't count the Wolfpack, and more than the Tremere. While STs that develop the city canonically can still add a lot more vampires of true clans, to me that indicates there can be a large population of Caitiff compared to the clans. Is Chicago normal or an outlier?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure how representative Chicago by Night really is, and how many of the Caitiffs there are really &quot;biological&quot; Caitiff, or a just not officially excepted onto a Clan because they were sired without permission.<br /> But even there the Caitiffs are all comparatively young, and have as Anarchs very little protection. I mean Gordon Keaton, Priscilla Gibbs and Neo all died in Under the Blood Red Moon. And Dickie's Fulcher ended up in topor.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T11:59:17-07:00","id":1534299,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534289#post1534289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure how representative Chicago by Night really is, and how many of the Caitiffs there are really &quot;biological&quot; Caitiff, or a just not officially excepted onto a Clan because they were sired without permission.</div> </div> </div> </div>All of the Caitiff are mechanical Caitiff. There are no outcast members of a clan who are actually real Brujah. Gangrel, etc. with the clan weakness. It's always been an issue with VtM that the term Caitiff is used for both exiled or abandoned members of that clan, and the actual clanless. Nevertheless in the city sourcebooks if a character is mentioned in the clan section, they are members of that clan. If there is an exception (like the Cathayan Effie Feng mentioned in Dark Colony who was pretending to be Malkavian, or the Lasombra characters in DC By Night pretending to be Ventrue) it is explicitly called out.<br /> <br /> I didn't just mention Chicago By Night, but multiple sourcebooks and explained which ones had many Caitiff and which ones did not, and what seemed to be the common reasons we might see that difference. There are domains with lots of Caitiff, and there are domains with very few Caitiff. STs will decide what seems to be more typical given how they design their city's power structure and how united and firm a hand the elders have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534289#post1534289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But even there the Caitiffs are all comparatively young, and have as Anarchs very little protection. I mean Gordon Keaton, Priscilla Gibbs and Neo all died in Under the Blood Red Moon. And Dickie's Fulcher ended up in topor.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think their age has any relevance in discussing their numbers. Most vampire, regardless of clan, are neonates. Unless you are talking about a specific historical period, and not the modern nights, I think it's immaterial.<br /> <br /> I also don't think the casualties of Under the Blood Red Moon are relevant either. CbN 2e lists 33 canonical character deaths. 5 Brujah, 4 Caitiff, 1 Gangrel, 4 Malkavians, 4 Nosferatu, 5 Toreador, 1 Tremere, and 7 Ventrue were destroyed. The Caitiff destroyed don't seem out of line for all the others. Now it's true that the Caitiff description in CbN 2e says the Lupines destroyed almost all of the city's clanless, but then introduces 3 new Caitiff that almost replaces the full number of canonical characters lost. On the other hand, we are given only 1 new Nosferatu and no new Toreador, Tremere (except for a Gargoyle), or Ventrue. So from a design perspective, this seems to be a conscious choice to keep the Caitiff numbers high.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T16:01:24-07:00","id":1534309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534205#post1534205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Giovanni are the smallest clan. Per original canon, they are only allowed three embraces every generation which is about one every decade. So a total of 50 new Giovanni have been embraced since the Promise of 1528. There are probably a few dozen Giovanni from earlier, but that is it. Even if we combine these numbers with a hundred or so surviving Cappadocians in whatever form (Samedi, Lamia, Harbingers, etc.) there's just not much left of the old Cappadocians. The Anarch Revolt and Giovanni purge hit them hard.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This number seems absurdly unreasonably small. I'm sure original canon said something like that but that would have to go not no consider these guys a bloodline.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3901-radiofreedeath","name":"RadioFreeDeath","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T18:10:59-07:00","id":1534313,"title":"","content":"The mention of only three family members chosen for the embrace every generation is in the original clan description in the original Players Guide and Vampires Player Guide 2e. However, I think the Giovanni clanbook ignored that and gave the impression of a much larger clan. I understand; if you are writing a clanbook you want to do what you can to expand things. But I think it's very clear that originally all four of the independent clans were meant to be rather small. They weren't meant to be broad archetypes but had a specific role in the setting. If you assume bloodlines are something like one or two dozen vampires at most, than a clan of 100-200 vampires (all factions collectively) is still significantly more. And the definition of clan doesn't seem to be based on size, but merely that they can legitimately point out they are descended from a third generation vampire and that the first of their &quot;kind&quot; isn't some weaker generation.<br /> <br /> But a lot of players seem to want the Giovanni/Hecata/Cappadocians (and the other independent clans too) to be fairly big clan so that's what they do in their games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T01:28:54-07:00","id":1534324,"title":"","content":"General vampire population numbers is one of those things that people get passionately very &quot;head canon&quot; about. For some reason they get twitchy when they realize that their grand storyline of vampire politics wouldn't happen in the first place because the mortal population to vampire population ratio is so far off it is beyond laughable.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;What do you mean my San Francisco chronical about a dozen coteries of Cainites fighting a dozen Wu of Kuei-jin doesn't make sense?&quot;<br /> &quot;With the mortal population of SF, you can have like five vampires total. Rather than a vampiric invasion, you are having five goths arguing in a room, but no one leaves because they don't want to be alone.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> Yes, I am one of those annoying people who brings up the fact that Kuei-Jin are in fact officially counted as vampires when the infamous 1:100,000 ratio gets brought up. Which means the total number of Cainites is actually about 1/3rd lower than what most people math it out to be.<br /> <br /> 8 billion mortals on the planet (1:100K ratio) means there are about 80,000 vampires in existence. There are about two Cainites for every Kuei-Jin, which results in about &gt;27K Kuei-Jin and about &gt;54K Cainites.<br /> <br /> There are 13 Clans and many Bloodlines, but keeping with simple calculations, just consider all the bloodlines to count as a 14th clan. That means each clan is only about 3,800 members strong worldwide, regardless of if they are active, in torpor, or are in other dimensions.<br /> <br /> That doesn't sound so bad at first glance, until you realize that a coterie/pack is between three and ten members strong, and most mixed vampire population cities have several coteries in residence on top of the Prince/Primogen/Sheriff group and then the Harpies and other vampires who are not in groups but are still part of the community. Your average ambitious vampire storyline ends up accounting for a huge portion of the vampire race.<br /> <br /> Of course, this gets even more silly when you go back in time. In 1800 the human population was only about 1 billion. Which means the Vampire population capped out at 10,000 in total. This then breaks down to about 6600 Cainites and about 3300 Kuei-jin. Doing the same 14-way split, that only leaves about &gt;500 members per clan worldwide, regardless of being awake, in torpor, or in another dimension.<br /> <br /> Amusingly enough by the math, your average vampire game should rarely have vampires in it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T02:48:05-07:00","id":1534326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534299#post1534299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't just mention Chicago By Night, but multiple sourcebooks and explained which ones had many Caitiff and which ones did not, and what seemed to be the common reasons we might see that difference. There are domains with lots of Caitiff, and there are domains with very few Caitiff. STs will decide what seems to be more typical given how they design their city's power structure and how united and firm a hand the elders have.</div> </div> </div> </div>The odd thing is the vampire population in the &quot;Night Books&quot; often doesn't really fit all that well with the general lore.<br /> <br /> In Chicago By Night 1sr edition the average generation of the NPCs was iirc roughly 8, with barely any kindred with a Gen in the double digits, and a pretty substantial number of them having Elder Generations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T12:49:58-07:00","id":1534361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534324#post1534324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">General vampire population numbers is one of those things that people get passionately very &quot;head canon&quot; about. For some reason they get twitchy when they realize that their grand storyline of vampire politics wouldn't happen in the first place because the mortal population to vampire population ratio is so far off it is beyond laughable.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;What do you mean my San Francisco chronical about a dozen coteries of Cainites fighting a dozen Wu of Kuei-jin doesn't make sense?&quot;<br /> &quot;With the mortal population of SF, you can have like five vampires total. Rather than a vampiric invasion, you are having five goths arguing in a room, but no one leaves because they don't want to be alone.&quot;</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Another good example would be Berlin, were you had to princes at War with each controlling one half of the city, just that each of them had iirc something like two or three at least somewhat loyal followers...<br /> <br /> And even if the each of the 30 or so vampires in the city would have been actually supporting one of the princes that would never be enough to actually keep vampires from the one part out of the other part of the city.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-21T04:04:52-07:00","id":1534518,"title":"","content":"I am from Vienna so I think the most numerous are the Nosferatu now followed by Caitiff and Tremere.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>As I am from Austria I need to clarify two things.<br /> First my native language is german and so please point out if the english I write is broken so I can improve.<br /> Second I do not own VTMV beyond first three books nor any line after M20 Corebook because it is not out there and I wait for the translation.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26160-koronus","name":"Koronus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-21T06:46:52-07:00","id":1534521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534361#post1534361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another good example would be Berlin, were you had to princes at War with each controlling one half of the city, just that each of them had iirc something like two or three at least somewhat loyal followers...<br /> <br /> And even if the each of the 30 or so vampires in the city would have been actually supporting one of the princes that would never be enough to actually keep vampires from the one part out of the other part of the city.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, I don't think that keeping the other side from you half of the city is necessary. A more reasonable goal would to keep them away from you prime hunting grounds and such in you half of the city or other specific places which are important to you. And perhaps keep an eye on the key human institutions and such based in your half of the city.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-21T22:38:53-07:00","id":1534542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gurkhal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534521#post1534521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be honest, I don't think that keeping the other side from you half of the city is necessary. A more reasonable goal would to keep them away from you prime hunting grounds and such in you half of the city or other specific places which are important to you. And perhaps keep an eye on the key human institutions and such based in your half of the city.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think even for that their numbers would have been way to small, appart from the fact both princes were Ventrue, meaning that they likely don't even have typical hunting grounds.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-21T23:08:03-07:00","id":1534545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534542#post1534542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think even for that their numbers would have been way to small, appart from the fact both princes were Ventrue, meaning that they likely don't even have typical hunting grounds.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I understood the situation that the two Princes were not he only Cainites in the city and that there were also other Cainites who might make use of traditional hunting grounds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-22T03:23:01-07:00","id":1534549,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gurkhal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534545#post1534545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I understood the situation that the two Princes were not he only Cainites in the city and that there were also other Cainites who might make use of traditional hunting grounds.</div> </div> </div> </div>But among the not that many Cainites were iirc (has been yeaes since I read that Book) two elders that were members of the Primogen in both parts of the city, several Sabbat Members, a Neonazi Anarch Gang and I also think some other vampires that didn't really care who was prince (like Nosferatus living in the Underground a Gangrel living in the woods with IIRC a ghouled Pack of Werewolfs and Malkavians).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-23T07:10:02-07:00","id":1534587,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534299#post1534299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I also don't think the casualties of Under the Blood Red Moon are relevant either. CbN 2e lists 33 canonical character deaths. 5 Brujah, 4 Caitiff, 1 Gangrel, 4 Malkavians, 4 Nosferatu, 5 Toreador, 1 Tremere, and 7 Ventrue were destroyed. The Caitiff destroyed don't seem out of line for all the others. Now it's true that the Caitiff description in CbN 2e says the Lupines destroyed almost all of the city's clanless, but then introduces 3 new Caitiff that almost replaces the full number of canonical characters lost. On the other hand, we are given only 1 new Nosferatu and no new Toreador, Tremere (except for a Gargoyle), or Ventrue. So from a design perspective, this seems to be a conscious choice to keep the Caitiff numbers high.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just curious, do many people actually use Chicago by Night 2e? From day one of getting it the choices they made on who died were completely ignored by me. Heck in my games Lodin stays in power under Helena, and the Brujah are basically wiped out. Not that how Chicago plays out in my games matter, but if you were in a Champaign, why would you just randomly flip over the board because they published (in my opinion an abomination of a) Module an an unwanted and unnecessary (in my opinion) update to the great sourcebook you were using?<br /> <br /> I always felt like Chicago by Night second edition was published by people who didn't like the reality of the original, and where everything was logically going. Menele was obviously felled by Prias's magic sword and never waking up based on him not coming out of torpor LONG before Helena, Critas was statistically a joke for a millennia old fifth gen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-23T13:06:52-07:00","id":1534602,"title":"","content":"Critas wouldn't be the first ancient that was given poor stats in the game. Isim Ur Baal who was embraced way back 4000+ BC was fairly under powered.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-23T15:44:59-07:00","id":1534608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vamps Like Us</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534587#post1534587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just curious, do many people actually use Chicago by Night 2e? From day one of getting it the choices they made on who died were completely ignored by me.</div> </div> </div> </div>From my experience, if people played in a game that originally used Chicago By Night 1e, they held to their own canon and didn't use CbN 2e at all. Though they might have used some of the new NPCs from it. In fact, I've never seen anyone actually use the Under a Blood Red Moon scenario in game. Most players found the rapid changes too disruptive. So not only are the results different, the Lupine attack never happens. Of course, even UBRM and CBN 2e state that the &quot;canonical&quot; destroyed characters are just an example and that every ST can decide a different list of survivors. That's always been the folly of &quot;metaplot&quot; in RPGs. The only canon that should exist is that at your gaming table. Revisiting the same local region to advance the timeline is almost always a mistake because players have already departed from the original status quo.<br /> <br /> However, there are many people who began their chronicle using Chicago By Night 2e because that was the only sourcebook available to them. CbN 1e went out of print very early, and for many years only CbN 2e was published. It was only later that White Wolf resold their early sourcebooks in combined supplements like the Chicago Chronicles, or that the inheritors of the IP could sell them in online stores as PDFs or POD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 04-24-2024, 03:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-23T17:51:14-07:00","id":1534610,"title":"","content":"That was my experience. I bought the 2end edition, but never used it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T07:35:26-07:00","id":1534635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534602#post1534602\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Critas wouldn't be the first ancient that was given poor stats in the game. Isim Ur Baal who was embraced way back 4000+ BC was fairly under powered.</div> </div> </div> </div> I\u2019m not saying this is because most vampires who survive long enough to become Methuselahs are NOT Adventure Mad Adrenaline Junky/Player Character types, but it wouldn\u2019t surprise me \u2026 (After all, most NPCs are blissfully ignorant of their role as antagonists or supporting characters in a tabletop RPG, even a majority of Malkavians!).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T12:47:42-07:00","id":1534654,"title":"","content":"But Critas stats are really a bit odd, he (as a Brujah) has just 1 Point of Celerity, no Potence and his only Discpline above 5 is Auspex, and in that he has also only 6 dots.<br /> <br /> And this guy is an almost 2500 year old 5th Gen Vampire.<br /> <br /> <br /> But NPC with poor stats aren't that rare in VTM imo.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T13:00:35-07:00","id":1534657,"title":"","content":"When it comes to vampire population numbers in any given city, you defintely need to use suspension of disbelief and generally ignore the 1 for every 100,000 mortal rule to make it a meaningful game as it would be some noted just a couple of kindred who easily avoid each other and not having any trouble.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T17:57:58-07:00","id":1534668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534657#post1534657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When it comes to vampire population numbers in any given city, you defintely need to use suspension of disbelief and generally ignore the 1 for every 100,000 mortal rule to make it a meaningful game as it would be some noted just a couple of kindred who easily avoid each other and not having any trouble.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree with the interpretation that one can only have a &quot;meaningful game&quot; by ignoring the population ratio. It would be fair to say that you can only run specific types of stories by ignoring the ratio, but those types of stories would work better in say Werewolf or Exalted rather than Vampire if going by fluff &amp; RAW.<br /> <br /> Vampires are supposed to be rare, most cities and towns won't have any vampires whatsoever. They are the focal points of influence, power, and to a certain extent corruption. Some STs tend to write the stories where legions of neonates war with each other, when in fact the majority of the wars should be fought between ghouls and normal mortal servitors. VtM is supposed to be a personal horror game rather than Call of Duty: Fang edition, the Blood Hunt videogame notwithstanding.<br /> <br /> I have run games where there are only the PC coterie and maybe three other vampires encountered in the whole chronicle, and it turned out great. I have also run games that upended most of the setting and had a great time doing it. The population ratio is a feature rather than a bug, though suppose many STs are more interested in running an Avengers Endgame rather than something more personal in nature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T18:37:06-07:00","id":1534669,"title":"","content":"Lets look at the third largest cities in Tennessee. Knoxville has a little under 200,000 people. If you wanted to run a game there assume you round up and it can accommodate 2 whole vampires. <br /> <br /> Look at the largest city of Nashville. It can have 6, 7 if you round up. I suppose Nashville could handle most gaming groups, but they would basically be all or most of the population.<br /> <br /> I lean into way more than 1 per hundred thousand, I don't think the number was thought out well.<br /> <br /> What if you lived in West Virginia? Sorry, no city in the state can house a vampire. Charleston, with a population of 49,736 mathematically would round down. People want to play where they are familiar I would think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T00:58:00-07:00","id":1534684,"title":"","content":"My Sabbat game is centered around El Paso and Juarez. On average 1 in 4 Sabbat is Lasombra, that's certainly going to be the case in Latin America and its periphery. Besides lots of Keepers I didn't make definitive decisions about Cainite demographics though. I just write NPCs as I need them or feel inspired to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2198-malkavian87","name":"Malkavian87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2198_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T07:43:38-07:00","id":1534690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vamps Like Us</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534669#post1534669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lets look at the third largest cities in Tennessee. Knoxville has a little under 200,000 people. If you wanted to run a game there assume you round up and it can accommodate 2 whole vampires.<br /> <br /> Look at the largest city of Nashville. It can have 6, 7 if you round up. I suppose Nashville could handle most gaming groups, but they would basically be all or most of the population.<br /> <br /> I lean into way more than 1 per hundred thousand, I don't think the number was thought out well.<br /> <br /> What if you lived in West Virginia? Sorry, no city in the state can house a vampire. Charleston, with a population of 49,736 mathematically would round down. People want to play where they are familiar I would think.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Remember to consider the population of the region as a whole and not just the individual urban centres: By my estimate West Virginia as a whole could support 17 or 18 Kindred and the Charleston Metropolitan Area (As opposed to Charleston alone) could support three or four of those on a permanent or semi-permanent basis.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d like to suggest that the 1:100,000 figure makes more sense as an encouragement for vampires to shift their base of operations from time to time instead of a hard-and-fast population limit (A ratio that gives you an idea of how large a population needs to be for a vampire\u2019s depredations to go mostly unnoticed for generations at a time, with the background weirdness of any World of Darkness population being able to camouflage Specifically Vampire weirdness).<br /> <br /> So Charleston, West Virginia could reasonably host a coterie-sized population or even a larger gathering of multiple vampires, but if too many of those vampires are in permanent residence for too long and \u2018eating local\u2019 then somebody is going to notice: torches and pitchforks will likely become involved at some point thereafter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T10:56:34-07:00","id":1534703,"title":"","content":"I feel like the game went out of it's way to make it easy to feed and go unnoticed. There is a reason so many of Chicago's Kindred had Dominate, even without the ability to cloud their memory the kiss brings on a euphoric state and licking the wound makes the evidence of the bite disappear. Seduce a drunk human and there is no reason to murder all your victims.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T11:11:12-07:00","id":1534706,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534324#post1534324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">General vampire population numbers is one of those things that people get passionately very &quot;head canon&quot; about. For some reason they get twitchy when they realize that their grand storyline of vampire politics wouldn't happen in the first place because the mortal population to vampire population ratio is so far off it is beyond laughable.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;What do you mean my San Francisco chronical about a dozen coteries of Cainites fighting a dozen Wu of Kuei-jin doesn't make sense?&quot;<br /> &quot;With the mortal population of SF, you can have like five vampires total. Rather than a vampiric invasion, you are having five goths arguing in a room, but no one leaves because they don't want to be alone.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> Yes, I am one of those annoying people who brings up the fact that Kuei-Jin are in fact officially counted as vampires when the infamous 1:100,000 ratio gets brought up. Which means the total number of Cainites is actually about 1/3rd lower than what most people math it out to be.<br /> <br /> 8 billion mortals on the planet (1:100K ratio) means there are about 80,000 vampires in existence. There are about two Cainites for every Kuei-Jin, which results in about &gt;27K Kuei-Jin and about &gt;54K Cainites.<br /> <br /> There are 13 Clans and many Bloodlines, but keeping with simple calculations, just consider all the bloodlines to count as a 14th clan. That means each clan is only about 3,800 members strong worldwide, regardless of if they are active, in torpor, or are in other dimensions.<br /> <br /> That doesn't sound so bad at first glance, until you realize that a coterie/pack is between three and ten members strong, and most mixed vampire population cities have several coteries in residence on top of the Prince/Primogen/Sheriff group and then the Harpies and other vampires who are not in groups but are still part of the community. Your average ambitious vampire storyline ends up accounting for a huge portion of the vampire race.<br /> <br /> Of course, this gets even more silly when you go back in time. In 1800 the human population was only about 1 billion. Which means the Vampire population capped out at 10,000 in total. This then breaks down to about 6600 Cainites and about 3300 Kuei-jin. Doing the same 14-way split, that only leaves about &gt;500 members per clan worldwide, regardless of being awake, in torpor, or in another dimension.<br /> <br /> Amusingly enough by the math, your average vampire game should rarely have vampires in it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait a minute. Weren\u2019t the Kuei-jin retconned out of existence in V5? So a Vampire game set in 2024 using V5 rules and lore would only include Cainites for that 1:100,000 ratio. Slightly more manageable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T12:13:46-07:00","id":1534711,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534690#post1534690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember to consider the population of the region as a whole and not just the individual urban centres:</div> </div> </div> </div>That's correct per the game. The 100,000 figure is supposed to be for the metropolitan area as a whole. So while Knoxville itself is only 200,000, the Knoxville metro area is almost 900,000. The game reflects that there are a lot of people who don't live in a specific city, but frequently travel there.<br /> <br /> Of course, any ration of vampires to humans is completely arbitrary. We have no idea how many people per vampire are needed in order to adequately allow a vampire to survive and be unknown. It's not based on any kind of realism, but simply a guide to assist STs in designing their own chronicles.<br /> <br /> Since many STs love to include many dozens, even hundreds of vampires, in their games, they ignore that ratio so they can include all the characters, clans, bloodlines, sects, vampires across a spread of generations, etc. that they want. That's a valid choice, but it isn't any less arbitrary or more realistic than the canonical ratio.<br /> <br /> In my experience, players really only need about 20-30 vampire NPCs for them to have the experience that they expect. Anything more than that is superfluous as players don't keep track of more NPCs than that. Extra NPCs tend to be superfluous. You could keep to canonical ratio and still have more vampire NPCs available simply by expanding the geographic scope of the game. Instead of placing all your vampire NPCs into one city, you just make other nearby cities part of the game by frequent overnight travel, conclaves, inter-domain politics, etc. The Nashville metro area can hold around 20 vampires canonically, but Memphis adds another 14, Knoxville 10, and Chattanooga 5. Add some Gangrel in the wilderness, and you can easily have a game with 50-60 or so vampires and still go by canon. Not to mention all the smaller metro areas in the state you could include. Most chronicles aren't like that, but there is no reason for that. There's a reason Dark Colony covers all of New England instead of being Boston By Night.<br /> <br /> Plus even the game itself acknowledges the established ratio always holds. That's just what the Inner Circle of the Camarilla wants enforced. Chicago had way more Kindred than expected because Lodin always had to deal with hostile Primogen limiting his power, dealing with lots of rivals, and ruling a domain whose size and population was probably too big. The Los Angeles free state obviously ignored it. All Sabbat domains ignore it. It isn't hard for an ST to justify why this domain has more vampires than normal and still retain something tied to canon.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T12:54:28-07:00","id":1534720,"title":"","content":"I agree that the 1 kindred for every 100,000 mortal rule wasn't well thought in regards on how many of the undead could be in a fairly large city.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T15:11:11-07:00","id":1534738,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534690#post1534690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember to consider the population of the region as a whole and not just the individual urban centres: By my estimate West Virginia as a whole could support 17 or 18 Kindred and the Charleston Metropolitan Area (As opposed to Charleston alone) could support three or four of those on a permanent or semi-permanent basis..</div> </div> </div> </div>While I agree this would help the population ratio, the problem is that then we run face first into the fluff that more or less states that shifters own the countryside to the point that many people on this forum have argued that vampires have a trouble using a freeway to leave a city, apparently they run games in such a way that patrols of shifters are sitting on over passes scanning every vehicle with &quot;sense wyrm&quot; or its equivalent.<br /> <br /> To be fair the writers never really dealt with the concept of the suburbs when it comes to vampires. They are too insular to have faceless community members that can go missing and they are too well kept up to allow hunting to be the default method for feeding. Suburbs are generally too new for the urban decay feel that VtM kind of leans on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-26T12:56:14-07:00","id":1534774,"title":"","content":"So in general how many kindred do you have in your cities? We thought that maybe having at best thirty max was a good solid number to work with and have a decent number of stories and conflicts with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-26T13:56:56-07:00","id":1534777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534711#post1534711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my experience, players really only need about 20-30 vampire NPCs for them to have the experience that they expect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Still I think it a bit of a different feeling if each member of the Primogen is just speaking for him self and about two other clan members, or if he represents 10 to 20 vampires.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-26T16:30:33-07:00","id":1534783,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534774#post1534774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So in general how many kindred do you have in your cities? We thought that maybe having at best thirty max was a good solid number to work with and have a decent number of stories and conflicts with.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my own case I ask what sort of story I am wanting to tell, then fill it out from there. After 25+ years of playing, the standard storylines of neonates raging against the system is a bit played out for me. To use video game concepts, at this point I prefer alternative starting points, different builds, and different play methods.<br /> <br /> Imagine you are a coterie of neonates who want to bypass local vampire politics, so you pool your Resource backgrounds, and buy a nice communal haven in a city without a vampire presence. The prince technically can't stop you from leaving their sphere of influence, and once outside the city their &quot;legal&quot; power more or less ends. The game is mostly about setting up a power base in the new town. This means that Backgrounds are more important to direct play than many stats or disciplines. The only non-coterie vampires you interact with is someone you know from the old city or if someone wants to make a play for your new home. Thats less than 10 vampires total for the majority of a chronicle.<br /> <br /> In a different game I ran, I had the would-be coterie embraced on a camping trip by a passing gangrel, she gave them the standard introductory speech and &quot;good luck&quot; slap on the rump and disappeared into the night. Well the PCs then had to figure out what to do since they were in an unclaimed zone of territory in the middle of several other small cities that each had their own power structures. Which group do you join, do you go full independent, do you just go into torpor and hope everything blows over? That game had something like 50+ vampire NPCs but each town only had like 10 or so cainites. Yes, the sire gangrel was Ennoia herself. No, they didn't figure this out until much later in the game, and all the experienced players were having nervous breakdowns at the thought of the how many vampires were going to be coming for them once that information got out.<br /> <br /> In a third game I ran the coterie as a Daughter of Cacophony &amp; Toreador mix that was a vampire anime-ish idol band. A player put me up to it, don't judge me <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />. The coterie was mobile going from city to city for their concerts. This meant tons of NPCs in the game, but they would only be there for a week or two before traveling to a new place. Plus given the cutesy vampire theme they more or less could feed live on stage and flaunt many rules because it was all an act. Each city reacted differently to the coterie with different groups being outraged, overjoyed, and others trying to use them for their own plans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-26T18:14:15-07:00","id":1534785,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534777#post1534777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still I think it a bit of a different feeling if each member of the Primogen is just speaking for him self and about two other clan members, or if he represents 10 to 20 vampires.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never believed that Primogen speak for anyone other than themselves. I dislike the idea (that seems based from LARP more than tabletop materials) that Primogen are essentially clan representatives. They aren't. They are just the powerful vampires in the city that force the Prince and other powerful vampires (the other Primogen) to take heed of their desires. They are not representatives. They are the powerful magnates and great lords that even the king must respect.<br /> <br /> Let's look at Chicago By Night as an example. Despite the Venture, objectively, being the most powerful clan collectively in Chicago, they have no Primogen. Though Ballard schemes to be accepted as one of them. On the other hand there are two Brujah Primogen (Critias and Tyler) and the history section makes it clear that until recently there were three Brujah Primogen (including Procet whom Tyler recently secretly killed). The Malkavians also don't have a Primogen. O'Leary isn't a Primogen though Lodin honors her for starting the Great Chicago Fire that enabled him to seize power. So two clans don't have representatives on the Primogen Council, and one clan has multiple Primogen. Of course, this early on even the idea that there are Primogen advising/controlling Lodin isn't well known and that knowledge is part of the city's secrets.<br /> <br /> Milwaukee By Night has a Council of Elders, but it's not quite the same thing as the Primogen. That Council was originally instituted by Hrothulf to help him run the city. So they were more like a collective Seneschal. After Hrothulf stepped down, the Council of Elders collectively ruled the city and elevate one of their own members as Prince. Since Mark Decker never attends any meeting as the Gangrel representative, there are fierce debates whether to replace him with another, but it doesn't necessarily need to be another Gangrel. The &quot;one council member per clan&quot; is clearly an artifact of local politics.<br /> <br /> In New Orleans By Night, there is a Prince's Council that seems to be equivalent to the Primogen. But there are only four members leaving three clans entirely represented. DC By Night only mentions four Primogen excluding three clans entirely.<br /> <br /> So the early sourcebooks show lots of domains with Primogens that have nothing to do with the clans being represented. It seems to be based more from the original MET books that institutionalized a lot of a domain's offices so players in LARP games had some position they could go after, and turned the office into some kind of clan representative (so they could have another office, being the whip).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-27T22:13:02-07:00","id":1534845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city#post1534152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a fun question that I was thinking of.<br /> <br /> Which clan in your game is the most numerous? In our game it's the Brujah, Gangrel, Nosferatu and some Caitiffs and Thin Bloods<br /> <br /> Which Clan is the smallest in your game? For ours it's the Ventrue, Toreador and, Malkavians that has the smallest numbers with 1 or 2 members at most</div> </div> </div> </div>It feels strange for me to read that Toreadors are few in numbers in your games. I recall that they are one of the more common and succesful Clans - mainstays of both the Camarilla and the wider VtM setting.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-28T14:00:08-07:00","id":1534859,"title":"","content":"To be fair Muad\u2019Dib this is an Anarch domain and we couldn\u2019t think of good number of Toreador would be there as being members of the Camarilla aids them so much. Just like there wouldn't be to many Tremere groups in the city as they are solidly Camarilla and see little use of being part of the Anarchs with the odd neonate or ancillia who saw the destruction of there central chantry and the loss of there leadership as a chance to go into a different direction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 04-29-2024, 03:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-30T04:41:11-07:00","id":1534937,"title":"","content":"I swear that &quot;1/100,000&quot; thing is the single most harmful bit of <i>fluff</i> -- yes, <i>fluff</i> -- White Wolf ever put to paper.<br /> <br /> It doesn't account for variations in population density. It doesn't account for local and regional economic development, or local culture. It doesn't account for surveillance, law enforcement, samaritans, or hunters of any stripe. It doesn't account for other fatsplats, if and when present.<br /> <br /> But most importantly, it doesn't account for clans in the area. It doesn't account for their organizational structure or ideology. It doesn't account for feeding discipline, influence, or ability to maintain the Masquerade. It doesn't account for age or generation. It doesn't account for predation pressure between vampires.<br /> <br /> The books go out of their way to point this out at nearly every opportunity, but it gets ignored in favor of the number as if cWoD is a crunch-heavily tactical/strategic RPG. It'd be better if whichever publisher owns the right to the IP this week disavows the number and puts &quot;IGNORE THIS&quot; in giant bold text up front during the worldbuilding chapter (like a lot of these people read that one). People need to stop fixating on this ridiculous, arbitrary, number and instead focus on building organic cities with vampire populations it can support without breaking suspension of disbelief. Y'know, the <i>point of this thread in the beginning</i>.<br /> <br /> Hell, Nashville came up. Nashville's greater metro area has a population not of 700,000 but 2,000,000. <i>That's</i> the number you look at -- the greater metro area population -- not just the urban core or suburbs within city limits. Uniquely American urban sprawl means a constant flux of commuters, whether that's work or weekend fun.<br /> <br /> &quot;By the numbers&quot;, Nashville &quot;can&quot; support 20 vampires. <i>Will</i> the city in the heart of the Bible Belt with a heavy law enforcement surveillance framework, multiple federal law enforcement agencies' field offices, within close proximity to multiple military bases, which is also one of the country's centers for health care and health care technology research and development, support a population of 20 vampires? Not bloody likely, unless each and every one of those vampires is at least an ancilla with strong feeding discipline and considerable experience covering their tracks.<br /> <br /> In this chronicle I'm currently running, I did a quick write-up for Nashville since vampires from it may factor in. You know what I put there? A Tremere chantry, two or three Toreador, and a Ventrue. That's ten or so Camarilla vampires, all of whom from clans noteworthy for both feeding discipline and ability to maintain the Masquerade. They're the ones able to exist under the Inquisition's nose after the city was swept and cleared.<br /> <br /> And they currently consider Nashville <i>overpopulated</i>. To the point they don't honor the Tradition of Hospitality, unless visitors are established Camarilla vampires on official sect business with writ from another city's Prince, or an Archon or higher.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-30T10:28:35-07:00","id":1534953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534937#post1534937\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I swear that &quot;1/100,000&quot; thing is the single most harmful bit of <i>fluff</i> -- yes, <i>fluff</i> -- White Wolf ever put to paper.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you think about the ramifications of about half the fluff in VtM, it breaks the setting. The 1:100k thing is just the sprinkles on top of much worse things.<br /> <br /> There isn't any kind of unified vampire society aspect, like what the Blade setting has with a language, writting system, and organized feeding areas. The VtM clans generally aren't cohesive enough to rule like the great houses do. Further more the fluff keeps pushing diverse bloodline cities, when no clan would allow such a thing in their city.<br /> <br /> The fluff says that there are the Traditions, but in truth there is no real way to enforce them or penalty if they are ignored. The same thing happens with Boons and the half assed concept that the harpies somehow enforce boons by gossiping.<br /> <br /> Vampires are supposed to be rare, but there is no mechanical reason for it. It's not like you have to get to a certain age, essence level, or blood potency level before you can sire a new vampire. So there is really no reason why the VtM setting doesn't turn into Stakeland or the Vampire Hunter D setting. Mages are limited by the rarity of awakening and shifters are restricted by the fact that they have to wait a normal generation for a fresh crop of kinfolk to be born and grow up before they have a chance at becoming a full shifter. Vampires have nothing keeping them in check other than theoretical attrition.<br /> <br /> Vampires are supposed to be automatically afraid of fire, but they are nocturnal creatures which can't see in the dark without very specific disciplines which most don't have. Before the invention of the electric lightbulb, how did they function? <br /> <br /> Vampires are supposed to be the masters of the city and human civilization, kept hemmed in by shifter death squads roaming the edges of the wilderness. Yet several shifters make the city their home and there is very little to no mechanical support for why a vampire would stay in a city as opposed to well stocked doomsday bunker or castle.<br /> <br /> The fluff assumes that vampires default to hunting random mortals, rather than having a form of Herd despite the latter option vastly cutting down on the potential for being ambushed, caught by hunters, or just stuff going wrong. The Anita Blake book series had all sorts of vampire society rules, titles, and other supporting concepts that surrounded the mortal followers of a vampire. VtM just kind of stares blankly at the idea from a vampire society standpoint.<br /> <br /> The fluff acts like the VtM setting is the undead form of authoritarianism that everyone wants to rage against with elders and high councils etc., but it is simultaneously the Lost Boys where the vampires are beholden to no one and roam the streets as they see fit.<br /> <br /> The problem is that if you are focused on good world building, you need consistency and logic. If you are making a game, you need variety and flexibility so that the gamers can have whatever they want. To use a food analogy, good world building is a five star restaurant that specializes in a specific cuisine, a game on the other hand is shopping mall food court which offers something for everyone, even if none of it is particularly good on its own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T00:01:14-07:00","id":1534986,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">1:100.000 vampires per human can make sense in a &quot;global scale&quot; as some cities could not have vampire in it due to the presence of dangers like hunters, werewolves, mages or being dominated by a tighly united pack of vampires who do not want to share.<br /> <br /> However the truth is that in local or city level settings this makes the game unplayable because there is simple no vampire society to interact. It is because this simple truth that supplements tend to ignore ratio saying X city is an exception like Chicago which is overpopulated, but if you take into account that this happens mostly with every supplement then you start to realize that exception has become the rule.<br /> <br /> Why? Because as much as we love TTRPG and specially the vampire genre the Truth is that the masquerade is very poorly designed from a mechanic perspective.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">The embrace has no cost of yearly limit which could lead to vampire becoming a Zombie problem especially with the sabbat over there.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">Everyone talks about vampire population but ghoul and thrall population is also another problem due to no limit to Vinculum or apropiate cost.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">Vampires cannot feed from humans without keeping the masquerade because their kiss is &quot;Memorable&quot; instead of mind wiping and only 3/13 have Dominate.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">Vampires cannot &quot;see in the dark&quot; without a protean 1 or serpentis 2 power which makes 11/13 clans blind for most of history as they are scared from fire.</span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">Personally my advice is to use the books as a suggestion and tweak the setting to something more consistent because masquerade vampire never made sense as the game mechanics where always contradictory, the thing is that the everything else from the political aspect of the game to the katanas and trenchcoats had enough cool factor that we just choose to ignore it in pursue of our happines.<br /> <br /> I am a lucky GM who has a reliable group of childhood friends who are interested in board games so I can use my own rules, but for someone else who has to seek groups online a possible solution is to port some rules from Requiem 2ED using masquerade as a base, it is not perfect but it fixes many stuff like all vampires having night vision or the embrace having a cost (I use XP, but Requiem cost is 1 point of humanity, i do not like it because it feels a very high price but at least is a solution).</span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T05:39:44-07:00","id":1534994,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leandro16</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534986#post1534986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everyone talks about vampire population but ghoul and thrall population is also another problem due to no limit to Vinculum or apropiate cost.</div> </div> </div> </div>Back in the day, I ran a game where the Players were split into two teams, one team was a Nosferatu Warren who controlled a town, while the other team was some Tzimisce who were trying to take the place over. The Nosferatu focused on their Spawning Pools to mass produce armies of mutant animal ghouls, while the Tzimisce focused on flesh crafting servitor ghouls. It was an experimental game to see if one could pull off an RTS/TBS style game using the WoD setting. What we found was that there isn't enough blood or people to make it game breaking at scale. <br /> <br /> A ghoul servitor is great as an administrator for daytime operations or day light security. But once direct combat starts, a ghoul burns through vitae at the same rate as vampires, especially when they get injured. Thus if you want to keep your ghouls alive, you can burn through huge amounts of vitae after every fight, let alone a prolonged battle or siege.<br /> <br /> If memory serves it worked out that even a Background Herd rating of five (60 vessels) was barely enough to keep a 20 ghoul squad functioning, with very little left over for the vampires to consume. The reason being is you have to put the vessels in rotation so they can be fed on every couple of nights. The vampire master pretty much just turns into a Blood to Vitae conversion machine.<br /> <br /> The flip side of the healing doctrine for ghouls is a pure cannon fodder mentality, which works well for animal ghoul hordes, but when it comes to human ghouls, you can depopulate an area to the point of a masquerade breach in surprisingly little time. This is also assuming you don't require a specific skill set from your ghoul and are just using them like vitae boosted pokemon.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong, I have made some really scary combat groups that leaned heavily on ghouls, but after the 10th ghoul or so you are spending more and more of your time seeing to their vitae levels for diminishing returns. If you aren't really rigged to support a large ghoul force, then the fall off number is closer to 5 ghouls rather than 10.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T13:27:41-07:00","id":1535037,"title":"","content":"Back in the hay day of WoD they made some GURPS tie-in books, including one for VtM. Some of the best of the GURPS material would include guidance to how to fine tune your game and mechanics for certain styles of play and genre and things like that (kinda like the levers in Scion 2e, now that I think of it). I\u2019ve often commented how much I\u2019d like something like that for WoD, a sort of storytellers took box, but now I wonder if there was anything like that in the old GURPS VtM book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T15:36:18-07:00","id":1535043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534994#post1534994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The flip side of the healing doctrine for ghouls is a pure cannon fodder mentality, which works well for animal ghoul hordes, but when it comes to human ghouls, you can depopulate an area to the point of a masquerade breach in surprisingly little time. This is also assuming you don't require a specific skill set from your ghoul and are just using them like vitae boosted pokemon.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">This is interesting</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">The ghoul fodder strategy is an actual thing in-universe with the Tzimisce making mutated war ghouls who are so masquerade breaching that they seem to be taken from the Prototype franchise (I am looking at you Andrei) or Ghoul hit squads from other clans and sects during wars, sieges and princedoom violent adcquisitions.<br /> <br /> The problem of having ridiculously low Embrace, Ghouling and blood bonding cost which is 1 Blood Point is that the only thing preventing a Population Nuke is Good Faith as every newly embraced vampire can build an army in very few nights.<br /> <br /> Ghouls rebelling against their master due to starving, bad treatment, overpopulation </span><span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">or creating multiple tracks for hunters are another extra problem that tends to snowball, because if your rival starts to create tons of ghouls you are likely to mimic this strategy to protect yourself during the day when you are vulnerable.</span><br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Lucida Sans Unicode\">The masquerade mostly survives based on a pure Good Faith system, a legal term that means something operates based on the assumption that everyone or the great majority will behave like an honorable member of society or &quot;with the proper diligence of Family Father&quot;.<br /> <br /> Expecting such behaviour is ridiculous in a society that is defined by the Machivelian and antisocial behaviour of their members who some go the point of renouncing to their moral mentality (Paths), and others behave like war criminals on daily basis and i am talking about the sabbat which given the scale of the conflict and their territory they control make a significant amount of the vampire population.<br /> <br /> Note that i am not against the existence of ghoul hit squads or the ocassional Tzimisce horror show, they give great flavour to the game. However they must have and adquisition cost otherwise the setting is simple unsubstainable which is why in my games the embrace, ghouling and blood bonding has a cost in terms of XP and there is a Yearly limit to how many you can produce based on Blood Potency and other factors like certain disciplines (Presence and Dominate).<br /> <br /> This homebrew means that ghouls and progeny are an investment which increases the managing aspects of the game. This limitations help prevent the population Nuke, while still allowing plots like making a coterie of thralls and neonates in secret to take over the prince like certain Nosferatu of Chicago, creating an empire controlled by ghouls as intermediaries, or having to fight a tzimisce ghoul army with the difference that this time the fiend is even more invested in the fight.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T22:33:03-07:00","id":1535051,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534785#post1534785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I never believed that Primogen speak for anyone other than themselves. I dislike the idea (that seems based from LARP more than tabletop materials) that Primogen are essentially clan representatives. They aren't. They are just the powerful vampires in the city that force the Prince and other powerful vampires (the other Primogen) to take heed of their desires. They are not representatives. They are the powerful magnates and great lords that even the king must respect.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem I see with that, is that there isn't really a point to having a Primogen in there first place, if Prince, Primogen and the other officials like the Sheriff make allready up a third or so of the vampires in the city.<br /> <br /> Even if the Primogen aren't clan representatives (even if in the more organized clans that's probaly automatically the case) it doesn't really make much sense to have ruling council, if there is no one to rule over.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T13:21:33-07:00","id":1535084,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aahz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535051#post1535051\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem I see with that, is that there isn't really a point to having a Primogen in there first place, if Prince, Primogen and the other officials like the Sheriff make allready up a third or so of the vampires in the city.<br /> <br /> Even if the Primogen aren't clan representatives (even if in the more organized clans that's probaly automatically the case) it doesn't really make much sense to have ruling council, if there is no one to rule over.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem here is that the books fail to explain clearly enough how Kindred society works. So players imagine a society that works mostly as the human ones from where the titles come from.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the precise numbers, vampires are meant to be few and insular. As a society, all but the most populous Domains should be less like a small city and more like an extended family.<br /> <br /> Vampires don't do hierarchies and dominion with the actual intent of forming a functional society. They do it out of the mere desire to show their inferiors who's boss.<br /> <br /> Looking at the Primogen, or even at a Prince, as an administrative institution is to miss the point. They're not. They're simply the strongest motherfuckers throwing their weight around.<br /> <br /> Primogen are either elders with close enough power to not want to risk life and limb fighting each other, or elders who can't take on the Prince individually, but banded together can do as a group.<br /> <br /> And that's all, they're just bullies. The institutions and titles are just nice trappings for their bullying, and they would do it even to show up over a single pleb.<br /> <br /> You can, correctly, say that even the books miss this point sometimes, and that's one of the big problems, but that's the bottom line of Kindred politics: it is about showing off who's stronger and using titles as a play of make believe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T16:07:59-07:00","id":1535091,"title":"","content":"Welcome back Montparnasse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T12:21:45-07:00","id":1535110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535084#post1535084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem here is that the books fail to explain clearly enough how Kindred society works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you think that it is a question of poor explanation or that the writers never built a proper design bible for vampire society and thus each new book just did whatever it wanted with the concept without considering the ramifications?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T16:38:30-07:00","id":1535116,"title":"","content":"I'd say that is a pretty good answer to your question Thoth as world building doesn't spring fully formed ever. I remember when I was playing Shadowrun back in the day and early books said that the California Free State was controlled by Japan lock, stock and smoking barrel. Then the book comes out and instead we have a group of semi independent regions and the powers of Japan only truly contral San Francisco. <br /> <br /> So why would this series be any different?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T00:45:59-07:00","id":1535132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535116#post1535116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say that is a pretty good answer to your question Thoth as world building doesn't spring fully formed ever. I remember when I was playing Shadowrun back in the day and early books said that the California Free State was controlled by Japan lock, stock and smoking barrel. Then the book comes out and instead we have a group of semi independent regions and the powers of Japan only truly contral San Francisco.<br /> <br /> So why would this series be any different?</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course nothing springs fully formed into being. A creator often over focuses on a specific aspect due to their personal interests, a good example being Tolkien over developing the languages of his world due to his linguistic background before getting to other aspects of world building.<br /> <br /> But as I have mentioned in other threads, while new clans, disciplines, and history have been consistently added to VtM over the editions, the core concepts regarding what vampire society consists of hasn't really been updated or reviewed since practically 1st edition when they got around to giving explanations of what the Sabbat, Independents, and Anarchs were. Even after five editions there really hasn't been much if any adjustment to these concepts or how the vampire society might change due to technology rapidly requiring vampire society to adjust to remain hidden.<br /> <br /> Thus I found it odd to suggest that successive writers all failed to explain vampire society &quot;correctly&quot; rather than assuming that each writer covered what they were interested in and never poked at the parts everyone just assumed was good enough when it really needed an upgrade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T14:58:55-07:00","id":1535150,"title":"","content":"I ain't saying cWoD is perfect nor impervious to criticism,<i> but...</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534953#post1534953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you think about the ramifications of about half the fluff in VtM, it breaks the setting. The 1:100k thing is just the sprinkles on top of much worse things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do agree here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fluff says that there are the Traditions, but in truth there is no real way to enforce them or penalty if they are ignored. </div> </div> </div>It also says the only real Tradition among vampires is &quot;don't get caught&quot;. Pretty much everything else is more like a guideline than a hard rule.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The same thing happens with Boons and the half assed concept that the harpies somehow enforce boons by gossiping. </div> </div> </div>I'm trying not to be flippant about this one, but half-assed is certainly a whole lot more ass than our contemporary real-world economic system, which is at best sixteenth-assed...and that's me giving it the benefit of the doubt. Harpies ain't out there bundling and securitizing bad boons, exchanging them for boons to neonates, and offering boons to themselves as insurance against neonates being unable to repay the boons they exchanged for the bundles of bad ones, for an example. Good gossip is certainly more reliable and operationally secure than the credit rating system, for certain.<br /> <br /> Broadly speaking, no economic system ever created in human history has ever been truly rational. It's always down to whatever nonsensical bullshit to which the lion's share of those in power can agree -- and by that I mean, whatever they can exploit the readiest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampires have nothing keeping them in check other than theoretical attrition. </div> </div> </div>Well, even if we did an ecological survey using established theories and mathematical models of population dynamics, intraspecific competition, predator-prey ratios, sustainability, and stability...at the end of the day we're still talking about mythical creatures, not real specimens whose behaviors and biologies can be studied and quantified. At some point, data points must by necessity be arbitrary just for the sake of applying those models.<br /> <br /> I grant with what we can presume from game mechanics someone could actually do that modeling, and it would surely be a fun thought experiment. But that's all it would be, and the real impact it would have for running a chronicle -- let alone a fun one -- would honestly be minimal. It really is just better to asspull for the sake of fun sometimes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampires are supposed to be automatically afraid of fire, but they are nocturnal creatures which can't see in the dark without very specific disciplines which most don't have. Before the invention of the electric lightbulb, how did they function? </div> </div> </div>This is a conceit of modern-day life. I live in a rural area, and I used to do a hell of a lot of camping. Without pervasive light pollution, it doesn't get so dark at night you cannot see. Human eyes aren't that bad; they had to not be, for us to have survived millions of years in the wild surrounded by nocturnal predators (and creepy-crawlies that wouldn't predate us, but wouldn't hesitate to kill us dead if we threatened them) in the first place.<br /> <br /> Indoors, sure, different story. But here game mechanics save us: Rotshreck really only rears its head when the fire poses risk (or is a surprise), and most vampires are going to have enough dice pool (even in older editions) to just auto-succeed the miniscule difficulties associated with candles and other similarly-sized fires. They won't like it, sure, but the idea of any and every vampire Rotshrecking by the mere presence of a candle is just an absurd prospect, not even supported by game mechanics as-is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yet several shifters make the city their home and there is very little to no mechanical support for why a vampire would stay in a city as opposed to well stocked doomsday bunker or castle. </div> </div> </div>There's no fluff to suggest they don't. Hell, the Tremere make your former suggestion their forte, and Tzimisce your latter theirs. I can't even remember which metaplot character it is at the moment, but I remember at least one major Camarilla player havens in a federal CoG bunker and never leaves, simply having mortals brought to them to feed. I almost want to say it was Karsh, but that doesn't feel right -- Karsh was stated to do the same at either Mount Weather (which granted is a CoG facility but still), the Pentagon, or the Cheyenne Mountain complex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fluff assumes that vampires default to hunting random mortals, rather than having a form of Herd despite the latter option vastly cutting down on the potential for being ambushed, caught by hunters, or just stuff going wrong. </div> </div> </div>Huh? That's...literally the entire point of the Herd background. It also just happens to be the case a vampire can't feed indefinitely from a herd, unless the herd's sizable enough to be a potential liability (to the Masquerade itself or exposure to rivals) in its own right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fluff acts like the VtM setting is the undead form of authoritarianism that everyone wants to rage against with elders and high councils etc., but it is simultaneously the Lost Boys where the vampires are beholden to no one and roam the streets as they see fit. </div> </div> </div>Those aren't mutually-exclusive. It's explained in the fluff, by high muckety-mucks simply not giving a shit until the little vamps start fucking with their schemes. At which point, the hammer gets dropped usually with the most extreme prejudice. Most of the stick-it-to-the-man-ishness just comes from <i>certain</i> sects and clans known for melodramatics and martyr fetishism.<br /> <br /> Again, it's not &quot;do this&quot; or &quot;don't do that&quot;, it's &quot;don't get caught&quot;.<br /> <br /> In the end, <i>could</i> cWoD benefit from a bit more structuring and guidelines on how worldbuilding works? For those of us who understand the setting is supposed to be a gigantic societal clusterfuck grey area...maybe? but on the other hand, we have to look at the borderline obsessive extremes to which players <i>do</i> take what breadcrumbs have been offered, like this stupid 100k:1 population ratio.<br /> <br /> In the end, is it really worth it? I'd honestly say no.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T23:26:27-07:00","id":1535160,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535150#post1535150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also says the only real Tradition among vampires is &quot;don't get caught&quot;. Pretty much everything else is more like a guideline than a hard rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that the Traditions are the basis for most Camarilla law, such as how the Prince uses &quot;Elders permission to sire&quot; to control the locals and call blood hunts on those who don't follow their rules. Yes it is very tongue and cheek to say &quot;don't get caught&quot;, but most of the game is designed to promote stupidity because drama makes for story hooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535150#post1535150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm trying not to be flippant about this one, but half-assed is certainly a whole lot more ass than our contemporary real-world economic system, which is at best sixteenth-assed...and that's me giving it the benefit of the doubt. Harpies ain't out there bundling and securitizing bad boons, exchanging them for boons to neonates, and offering boons to themselves as insurance against neonates being unable to repay the boons they exchanged for the bundles of bad ones, for an example. Good gossip is certainly more reliable and operationally secure than the credit rating system, for certain.</div> </div> </div> </div>A credit rating is a record of how you have behaved financially over time, or to put in social terms a Financial Reputation. Using rumor mongering to destroy reputations is one of the older tricks in the book, which functionally is not much different than identity theft to burn someone else's Credit Score. So there is not much difference between the two concepts, except that most gossip is fake or detrimental rather than being accurate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535150#post1535150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, even if we did an ecological survey using established theories and mathematical models of population dynamics, intraspecific competition, predator-prey ratios, sustainability, and stability...at the end of the day we're still talking about mythical creatures, not real specimens whose behaviors and biologies can be studied and quantified. At some point, data points must by necessity be arbitrary just for the sake of applying those models.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or you could just remember that Vampires despite all the emo aesthetic would actually be classified as Parasites rather than Predators. Immortal tape worms of society which despite the minority of them killing for fun or when feeding, actually fall into one of the more symbiotic sub-categories of parasitism. You can take your pick of aspects which are deemed beneficial, the fact that any vampire can heal mortals with vitae, the fact that Dominate is a therapists dream ability, the fact that vampires are living time capsules of knowledge and experience that society often forgets and loses.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535150#post1535150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indoors, sure, different story. But here game mechanics save us: Rotshreck really only rears its head when the fire poses risk (or is a surprise), and most vampires are going to have enough dice pool (even in older editions) to just auto-succeed the miniscule difficulties associated with candles and other similarly-sized fires. They won't like it, sure, but the idea of any and every vampire Rotshrecking by the mere presence of a candle is just an absurd prospect, not even supported by game mechanics as-is.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>V20 page 299</b><br /> <u>Provocation Difficulty</u><br /> Lighting a cigarette 3<br /> Sight of a torch 5<br /> Bonfire 6<br /> Obscured sunlight 7<br /> Being burned 7<br /> Direct sunlight 8<br /> Trapped in burning building 9\u200b </div> </div> </div>The first four examples present no direct danger, and specifically the &quot;sight of a torch&quot; is particularly damning since there is no range requirement/limitation. This makes for the action of walking down a hallway lit by torches mechanically speaking problematic. This is the same concept of rolling dex every time you tie your shoelaces, it is silly and time consuming, but eventually the RNG will strike and now you have to explain why a vampire who has run their own blacksmith shop has absolutely lost it due to someone lighting a candle in their vicinity. If the intention was to not bog down play with such things, then the rules would be more along the lines of &quot;when vampire is set on fire, roll to avoid Rotschreck based on how big the fire is&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T16:23:08-07:00","id":1535203,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535160#post1535160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except that the Traditions are the basis for most Camarilla law, such as how the Prince uses &quot;Elders permission to sire&quot; to control the locals and call blood hunts on those who don't follow their rules. Yes it is very tongue and cheek to say &quot;don't get caught&quot;, but most of the game is designed to promote stupidity because drama makes for story hooks.</div> </div> </div> </div>...yeah?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A credit rating is a record of how you have behaved financially over time, or to put in social terms a Financial Reputation. Using rumor mongering to destroy reputations is one of the older tricks in the book, which functionally is not much different than identity theft to burn someone else's Credit Score. So there is not much difference between the two concepts, except that most gossip is fake or detrimental rather than being accurate. </div> </div> </div>That's actually not what credit rating is at all, but that's a tangential (and political) conversation. Rumor mongering to destroy reputations is actually closer to how credit agencies reduce scores for fiscally-responsible borrowers, than it is identity theft.<br /> <br /> Either way, prestation in the World of Darkness is <i>still</i> a more rational, transparent, and reliable, and less-predatory, system than the one we all live under in the real world.<br /> <br /> [quote]<br /> Or you could just remember that Vampires despite all the emo aesthetic would actually be classified as Parasites rather than Predators. Immortal tape worms of society which despite the minority of them killing for fun or when feeding, actually fall into one of the more symbiotic sub-categories of parasitism. You can take your pick of aspects which are deemed beneficial, the fact that any vampire can heal mortals with vitae, the fact that Dominate is a therapists dream ability, the fact that vampires are living time capsules of knowledge and experience that society often forgets and loses.<br /> [quote]<br /> This counters my point how? <i>Even if</i> we substituted &quot;predator-prey&quot; for &quot;parasite-host&quot; in my little discussion about ecological studies, it still holds true: we're talking about mythical creatures. We could still do the numbers based on real studies of parasite-host interacts as they've been done, and it would still be completely meaningless as we're talking about mythical creatures.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The first four examples present no direct danger, and specifically the &quot;sight of a torch&quot; is particularly damning since there is no range requirement/limitation. This makes for the action of walking down a hallway lit by torches mechanically speaking problematic. This is the same concept of rolling dex every time you tie your shoelaces, it is silly and time consuming, but eventually the RNG will strike and now you have to explain why a vampire who has run their own blacksmith shop has absolutely lost it due to someone lighting a candle in their vicinity. If the intention was to not bog down play with such things, then the rules would be more along the lines of &quot;when vampire is set on fire, roll to avoid Rotschreck based on how big the fire is&quot; </div> </div> </div>Also from the V20 corebook, also page 299,<br /> <br /> <i>Relatively innocuous stimuli, or stimuli directly under the character\u2019s control, are unlikely to induce R\u00f6tschreck. For example, a character who sees a lit cigarette in a nightclub, or a screened-in fireplace in an ally\u2019s home, might grow uneasy, but is unlikely to succumb to the Red Fear. If that same cigarette is pointed threateningly at the vampire, though, or the fireplace suddenly flares up\u2026</i><br /> <br /> And DAV20, page 358,<br /> <br /> <i>Innocuous stimuli directly under the character\u2019s control are unlikely to induce R\u00f6tschreck. However, a lamp waved threateningly or fireplace suddenly flaring up may call for a roll.</i><br /> <br /> You gotta read the &quot;not the difficulty charts&quot; part of the text. That <i>is</i> the rule. No, a simple hallway lit by torches will not induce Rotschreck, that is the rote definition of an innocuous source.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T11:29:36-07:00","id":1535222,"title":"","content":"<i>For example, a character who sees a lit cigarette in a nightclub, or a screened-in fireplace in an ally\u2019s home, might grow uneasy, but is unlikely to succumb to the Red Fear.</i><br /> <br /> You could take this as the target number being low so most likely the vampire would not fail his roll. It does not say he wont, only that is the more unlikely outcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24221-vamps-like-us","name":"Vamps Like Us","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T18:39:08-07:00","id":1535237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vamps Like Us</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535222#post1535222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>For example, a character who sees a lit cigarette in a nightclub, or a screened-in fireplace in an ally\u2019s home, might grow uneasy, but is unlikely to succumb to the Red Fear.</i><br /> <br /> You could take this as the target number being low so most likely the vampire would not fail his roll. It does not say he wont, only that is the more unlikely outcome.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an example; the dispositive language in the excerpt is &quot;innocuous source&quot;, &quot;directly under the character's control&quot;, and &quot;induce Rotschreck&quot;. A source of fire being directly under a character's control is self-explanatory, but the definition of innocuous is &quot;harmless or inoffensive&quot;; in other words, fire is only going to induce Rotschreck if it could potentially harm the vampire. That's an entirely different phenomenon than <i>resisting</i> Rotschreck; it is induced regardless, it is up to the vampire to <i>resist</i> it once induced.<br /> <br /> In other words, to even call for a check to resist Rotschreck in the first place, the fire must be in a position or context to cause potential harm to the vampire. A burning torch or candle secure in a sconce is not in a position or context in which it could cause potential harm; it's not moving, it's not flaring, it's just quietly burning shedding light and heat. Vampires will certainly be conscious of it and likely stay away due to risk and psychological aversion, but it will not induce Rotschreck. Only if it is taken and wielded by someone hostile to the vampire, would it induce Rotschreck and force a resistance check.<br /> <br /> Storytellers should always ask themselves &quot;does this fire pose a threat to the character?&quot; <i>before</i> calling for the Rotschreck test. If no, it cannot induce Rotschreck, and therefore calling for the roll is inappropriate. Instead, the storyteller should narrate the scene with a mind towards highlighting discomfort, and mere discomfort does not provoke Frenzy any of type, whether that's hunger, fury, <i>or</i> fear.<br /> <br /> Not to put too fine a point on this, but we're not making your average player or average clan roll Frenzy checks any time a Harpy opens their mouth or upon sight of an enemy or rival, on the first and every subsequent combat round, blood pool/hunger dips below optimal, seeing a sunrise on a television screen, or at the mere sight of a crucifix. Because not every stimulus that might be broadly construed as a <i>possible</i> Frenzy trigger, is guaranteed to trigger Frenzy. Fire is absolutely no different than any other, just by merit of being fire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 05-07-2024, 09:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T19:24:59-07:00","id":1535240,"title":"","content":"Nowadays I find the whole thing of fire being a weakness to Vampires quite stupid.<br /> <br /> It has no ground on myth or fiction. The nearest related myth is just about burning corpses suspect of being vampires, and even then there's nothing said that they burn easier than common corpses (which are actually quite hard to burn).<br /> <br /> Basically the only source on vampires that state that fire is their bane is... this game. I'm going to ditch it on my Requiem games and maybe in Masquerade too, and just add another more interesting and less ubiquitous bane if it gets too OP.<br /> <br /> That said: a nocturnal predator without any sense geared towards navigating darkness is both stupid and sad. And it doesn't matter if natural light at night isn't that bad, their prey dwell on caves, natural or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T21:41:56-07:00","id":1535251,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535240#post1535240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nowadays I find the whole thing of fire being a weakness to Vampires quite stupid.<br /> <br /> It has no ground on myth or fiction. The nearest related myth is just about burning corpses suspect of being vampires, and even then there's nothing said that they burn easier than common corpses (which are actually quite hard to burn).<br /> <br /> Basically the only source on vampires that state that fire is their bane is... this game. I'm going to ditch it on my Requiem games and maybe in Masquerade too, and just add another more interesting and less ubiquitous bane if it gets too OP.<br /> <br /> That said: a nocturnal predator without any sense geared towards navigating darkness is both stupid and sad. And it doesn't matter if natural light at night isn't that bad, their prey dwell on caves, natural or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;stumbling vampire&quot; had a very hard life, at one point stumbling in the dark, and at another facing the fear of God from torches and candles, all the way from the stone age up to the modern era until humans invented the electrical lamps...<br /> <br /> The only ever other source about fire harming vampires that I could think of is from Anne Rice Vampires. Louis burns down all vampires of the theater to ashes after all. But the Vampires there don't fear fire, regardless of that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T12:42:46-07:00","id":1535294,"title":"","content":"It's been awhile since I read the Anne Rice books, but if I remember that fire was deadly or very dangerous to the very young and weak vampires and that couldn't heal from them easily in general. However the older and much stronger ones had less to fear from fire damage and in some causes would do no damage. Maybe just go with that in your games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T14:01:21-07:00","id":1535300,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535251#post1535251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The &quot;stumbling vampire&quot; had a very hard life, at one point stumbling in the dark, and at another facing the fear of God from torches and candles, all the way from the stone age up to the modern era until humans invented the electrical lamps...<br /> <br /> The only ever other source about fire harming vampires that I could think of is from Anne Rice Vampires. Louis burns down all vampires of the theater to ashes after all. But the Vampires there don't fear fire, regardless of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Louis burns the whole building to the ground, it was less &quot;they're weak to fire&quot; and more &quot;they're weak to total annihilation&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T20:02:49-07:00","id":1535322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535300#post1535300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Louis burns the whole building to the ground, it was less &quot;they're weak to fire&quot; and more &quot;they're weak to total annihilation&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>And some he decapitated on top of that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-09T00:38:52-07:00","id":1535336,"title":"","content":"From a science based perspective I always rationalized that fire, acid and other extreme conditions cauterizes the wounds making them harder to heal, because it counts as &quot;an already healed wound&quot; or a scar.<br /> <br /> From a magical perspective i tell my players that fire is strong against most supernaturals because it has metaphysical weight as the weapon of humans, who by taming fire where able to beat the darkness and advance into the civilization we live today. Fire is also the symbol of Prometheus, and thus represents the latent potential of humanity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-09T23:30:18-07:00","id":1535401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leandro16</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535336#post1535336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From a science based perspective I always rationalized that fire, acid and other extreme conditions cauterizes the wounds making them harder to heal, because it counts as &quot;an already healed wound&quot; or a scar.<br /> <br /> From a magical perspective i tell my players that fire is strong against most supernaturals because it has metaphysical weight as the weapon of humans, who by taming fire where able to beat the darkness and advance into the civilization we live today. Fire is also the symbol of Prometheus, and thus represents the latent potential of humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Brown Falcon: &quot;Should we tell them?&quot;<br /> <br /> Black Kite: &quot;Nah. Leaves 'em guessing.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-16T06:50:07-07:00","id":1535723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1534657#post1534657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When it comes to vampire population numbers in any given city, you defintely need to use suspension of disbelief and generally ignore the 1 for every 100,000 mortal rule to make it a meaningful game as it would be some noted just a couple of kindred who easily avoid each other and not having any trouble.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that you can't apply the rule the way a lot of people want to and have interesting interactions. <br /> <br /> I like to think that *on average* something like the 1:100,000 rule applies, but that vampires are clustered. So the greater LA area has like 18 million people, and can support 180 vampires theoretically, but that doesn't mean that every city (the area has dozens of &quot;cities&quot;) is going to have the 1:100K ratio. Downtown LA is going to have a ratio of like 1:20,000. Santa Monica is going to have a ratio of like 1:25,000. Long Beach is going to have a ratio of like 1:30,000. But Ventura County is going to have like 1 or 2 vampires for the entire population of 800,000. Riverside-San Bernardino has like 3 million people, but maybe it only has like 6-7 vampires.<br /> <br /> The vampires are going to go where the ACTION is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13136-legendre","name":"Legendre","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"328"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-16T07:00:31-07:00","id":1535724,"title":"","content":"Getting back to the original post for just a second....<br /> <br /> My L.A. game has populations of:<br /> <br /> Brujah: ~20 (2 younger elders, 3 powerful ancillae, and a fuckton of neonates)<br /> Gangrel: ~11 (3 younger elders, 2 powerful ancillae, 2 average ancillae, 4 neonates)<br /> Malkavians: ~11 (1 serious elder, 3 powerful ancillae, 7 neonates)<br /> Nosferatu: ~12 (1 elder, 1 young elder, 1 powerful ancilla, 1 ancilla, 8 neonates)<br /> Toreador: ~17 (4 younger elders, 4 ancillae, 9 neonates)<br /> Tremere: ~16 (1 elder, 2 younger elders, 3 ancillae, 2 younger ancillae, 8 neonates)<br /> Ventrue: ~22 (2 elders, 3 younger elders, 4 ancillae, 13 neonates)<br /> Setites: ~1 (neonate)<br /> Caitiff: 0<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13136-legendre","name":"Legendre","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"328"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-16T13:09:19-07:00","id":1535746,"title":"","content":"A good breakdown Legendre.<br /> <br /> Our game has the following so far.<br /> <br /> Brujah: 5 ( 1 potent elder, 2 older ancillae and 2 neonates)<br /> <br /> Tremere: 3 ( 2 powerful ancillae and 1 older neonate)<br /> <br /> Gangrel: 8 ( 2 younger ancillae and the others are a mishmash of young &amp; older neonates)<br /> <br /> Malkavian: 1 ( an older ancillae and nobody else, but that could change in the future)<br /> <br /> Nosferatu: 10 ( 1 dangerous elder, 2 influenctial older ancillae, 7 neonates)<br /> <br /> Ventrue: 3 ( 1 ambitious ancillae and her older neonate childe also 1 dangerous older ancillae who has a big grudge with the Brujah and especially ones that trace there lineage to Tyler which includes the Baron in our game)<br /> <br /> Ministry: 2 ( 2 older neonates)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 05-17-2024, 03:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-18T07:43:24-07:00","id":1535852,"title":"","content":"Well, for what it's worth I'll share my current chronicle's population...<br /> <br /> Louisville, KY, is kind of a shitshow right now. SI wiped out the Cams and only a handful made it out, and the Anarchs moved in during the Breonna Taylor protests. The Prince is a Camarilla-aligned Hecata, and is the de facto leader of an Hecata conspiracy to wipe out the Anarchs if they attempt to revolt against her. As a result the city is heavily overpopulated, and the first part of the chronicle is about who is going to come out on top. As far as the Inquisition goes, the situation is <i>largely</i> stable as most of the city's vampires are disciplined feeders.<br /> <br /> Not accounting for player characters:<br /> <br /> Political distribution...<br /> <br /> Camarilla: Toreador (1), Gangrel (1), Tremere (1), Nosferatu (1), Malkavian (1, currently still in torpor), Caitiff (1, newly moved in from Nashville), Ventrue (1, former Prince, thought dead but actually being held captive by the Inquisition).<br /> <br /> Anarch: Ministry (2), Brujah (3), Ventrue (1), Nosferatu (3), Gangrel (1).<br /> <br /> Hecata: Nagaraja (1, Prince), Harbinger (1), Milliner (1), Puttanesca (1).<br /> <br /> Age distribution...<br /> <br /> The Harbinger is <i>technically</i> a methuselah, but spent most of that time as a wraith before being re-embodied. Due to that fuckery, he's only 10th generation and BP 2, but has the discipline levels and spread of a young elder. But for the moment, he's more interested in learning about contemporary society and generally fucking around than being active in the Jyhad.<br /> <br /> The former Prince is the only true elder left in the city. She's a moderate-generation (8th) mid-level elder, being about 600 years old. But she's incredibly handicapped in capability, because she's a blood potency-capped consensualist.<br /> <br /> One of the Ministry vampires is a mid-level ancilla, 10th generation and about 200 years old (BP 3). The Nagaraja and Anarch Gangrel are mid-level ancillae too, they're both about a century old and 11th generation; the Nagaraja is BP 2 and the Gangrel is BP 1.<br /> <br /> The Toreador and one of the Anarch Brujah are young ancillae, both being embraced during WWII. They're both 12th generation.<br /> <br /> Everyone else is a neonate, but the Camarilla Nosferatu is on the cusp of being considered ancilla as he was embraced in the '50s.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-18T11:49:37-07:00","id":1535859,"title":"","content":"Our city is an Anarch domain mainly controlled by the Brujah, Gangrel and Nosferatu clans and there was never a strong Camarilla presence as there presence was further up north.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-18T14:30:25-07:00","id":1535866,"title":"","content":"My chronicle's a continuation/adaptation/mix-and-match of older chronicles' timelines (with a new group). In that timeline, Louisville was seized by the Camarilla during the Civil War, waging a war alongside a loose coalition of independent and autarch elders aligned with the Southern Lords, cheekily named in retrospect the &quot;Antebelluvians&quot; by locals. The Ventrue led the war effort as Dux Bellorum of all domains south of the Ohio river, and she made Louisville her own little social experiment, only granting veteran Kindred domain and station (and only allowing those who had experienced war the right to stay) in the city. Think the UCF from Starship Troopers (the novel, not the film), but as a Camarilla city.<br /> <br /> The Ventrue is Nova Arpad's childe and Bulscu's protege, embraced in 1457 after the Siege/Miracle of Belgrade*, and she was a crusader-turned-Archon until her appointment as Dux Bellorum. Needless to say, to call her the Camarilla's 800-pound gorilla in the region may be an understatement...to the Camarilla's chagrin, as she rolls <i>hard</i> on the &quot;Lawful Good+, D&amp;D Paladin&quot; archetype. To the point she managed to broker a treaty with the Catholic Church and Society of Leopold, for Louisville's Camarilla to police its own for low-Humanity, rogue, and hostile vampires.<br /> <br /> <i>[* From my Transylvania Chronicles game. The Miracle of Belgrade happened during the peak of the battle in 1456, vampires defending the city who followed the Roads of Humanity, Kings, and Heaven found their curse of Caine lifted for a single day, able to continue fighting at full strength without getting burned by the Sun. This Ventrue was a peasant girl and a camp follower of John of Capistrano's peasant-crusaders, who ended up fighting alongside Bulscu and saving him from an Assamite's sneak attack. She impressed Bulscu, who in my TC game rose from torpor and went full-on &quot;good guy&quot;, who arranged to have her embraced by Nova as reward.]</i><br /> <br /> The Nagaraja was a nurse in WWI, would have died from the Spanish flu but got embraced, and went back to Europe for a second round in WWII by dominating her way into the nurse corps. The ancilla Brujah was a Soviet commissar (he was Primogen until the SI hit Louisville, at which point he defected to Anarchs believing the SI hit to be an inside job), the Toreador was a Spanish republican turned French resistance who seduced Nazi officers for intelligence. The Gangrel's brother fought in Vietnam, the Tremere's mortal boyfriend died in Vietnam, and the Malkavian served a tour in Vietnam; all three went onto protest Vietnam. The Nosferatu and Caitiff are newer entrants to the city (under the Nagaraja, not the Ventrue), but the Nosferatu got involved in the Mexican Dirty War.<br /> <br /> That much military expertise and combat experience made Louisville one tough motherfucker for the Sabbat, who besieged the city multiple times but never came close to taking it. The best the Sabbat (and surviving Antebelluvians) could do was guerilla warfare type shit, leading to the 2008 Kentucky Derby Masquerade breach I've mentioned in other threads. That's what put Louisville on the SI's radar, eventually leading to the hit. The SI in Louisville is comprised of <i>Protestants</i> and military, rather than Catholics and intelligence agencies. The hit <i>was</i> an inside job, but by the former Malkavian Primogen; he had a premonition the only way to save Louisville in the long run was to let the SI clean house, and leaked the location of Elysium to them.<br /> <br /> So, you have a half dozen ancillae (and old neonates) who were all hardened by war in life, and went on to become sect war veterans, up against nearly a dozen Anarchs only three of whom have any significant age.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-22T12:36:24-07:00","id":1536112,"title":"","content":"If we are going by age breakdown. In our city we have two elders that were embraced early in the 18th century AD, six older ancilliae who were embraced between 1782-1855 AD, four younger ancilliae who were embraced between 1870-1920 AD, five older neonates that were embraced 1950-1989 AD and maybe four neonates that were embraced in the last decade or so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-10T07:05:35-08:00","id":1557450,"title":"","content":"I'm of the mind that Nosferatu would always be the largest population of any clan for the following reasons.<br /> <br /> 1 - Information brokers, means they'd need to keep tabs on as many areas as possible.<br /> <br /> 2 - Nobody comes to visit unless they desperately need something. Which means the clan is probably able to get away with illegal embraces a lot easier.<br /> <br /> ------------------------<br /> <br /> After them, it would probably be a toss up between Brujah and Toreador for second most numerous. They represent the most common archetypal tropes for modern vampires, after all. Plus, their tendency to embrace on whims of passion.<br /> <br /> ------------------------<br /> <br /> Coming in third would be the Ventrue, because somebody needs to make sure the city doesn't fall to chaos. Unfortunately, there's only so many authoritative positions to go around, and no self respecting Ventrue would be satisfied working as someone else's subordinate.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------<br /> <br /> For least populated, it would be the Gangrel, Tremere and Caitiff.<br /> <br /> <br /> Gangrel, because it is within their nature to stay on the move and typically avoid industrialized society.<br /> <br /> Tremere, because who the hell is practicing hermetic blood magic in this day and age?<br /> <br /> Caitiff, because the ones who were abandoned by their sires are probably executed as illegal embraces.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-10T18:47:05-08:00","id":1557460,"title":"","content":"Clan-domination of a city is more of a neonate-thing; vampires, <i>do </i>travel and ancilla and older, will surely move to greener-pastures; I'm sure vampire covens start as such, in the New World, and perhaps in the Old World, like new Roman-towns in newly conquered Gaul. A vampire leaves his home city, and establishes a haven, then, creates a few neonates, then, they drift in time, and others' drift in. <br /> <br /> The one thing about vampires in Masquerade and Requiem, is, they are dependent on which ever Clan was their sire is, determines their powers/weakness etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-11T05:42:55-08:00","id":1557467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557460#post1557460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clan-domination of a city is more of a neonate-thing; vampires, <i>do </i>travel and ancilla and older, will surely move to greener-pastures; I'm sure vampire covens start as such, in the New World, and perhaps in the Old World, like new Roman-towns in newly conquered Gaul. A vampire leaves his home city, and establishes a haven, then, creates a few neonates, then, they drift in time, and others' drift in.<br /> <br /> The one thing about vampires in Masquerade and Requiem, is, they are dependent on which ever Clan was their sire is, determines their powers/weakness etc.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> While they do travel, it's made pretty clear that doing so is considerably more difficult for them than it is for humans. There's all kinds of precautions and protocols that need to be dealt with, before they can pack up and leave.<br /> <br /> Not least of which involves making sure the vampire government is even going to let you settle down once you step foot in their territory. I seriously doubt the local prince is going to step down off his throne and hand it over, just because you were sitting on top back in your previous domain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-12T08:09:05-08:00","id":1557507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557450#post1557450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm of the mind that Nosferatu would always be the largest population of any clan for the following reasons.<br /> <br /> 1 - Information brokers, means they'd need to keep tabs on as many areas as possible.<br /> <br /> 2 - Nobody comes to visit unless they desperately need something. Which means the clan is probably able to get away with illegal embraces a lot easier.<br /> <br /> ------------------------<br /> <br /> After them, it would probably be a toss up between Brujah and Toreador for second most numerous. They represent the most common archetypal tropes for modern vampires, after all. Plus, their tendency to embrace on whims of passion.<br /> <br /> ------------------------<br /> <br /> Coming in third would be the Ventrue, because somebody needs to make sure the city doesn't fall to chaos. Unfortunately, there's only so many authoritative positions to go around, and no self respecting Ventrue would be satisfied working as someone else's subordinate.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------<br /> <br /> For least populated, it would be the Gangrel, Tremere and Caitiff.<br /> <br /> <br /> Gangrel, because it is within their nature to stay on the move and typically avoid industrialized society.<br /> <br /> Tremere, because who the hell is practicing hermetic blood magic in this day and age?<br /> <br /> Caitiff, because the ones who were abandoned by their sires are probably executed as illegal embraces.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Some thoughts that occurred to me with regard to this post:-<br /> <br /> - It makes sense that Nosferatu would be able to evade scrutiny to a greater degree than most Clans, but I would argue that their preference for going undetected strongly suggest that they would work to keep their number of Embraced down: it might be wiser to depict Clan Nosferatu as generally well-represented not because they make<br /> more vampires, but because more of the ones they make survive.<br /> <br /> <br /> - I\u2019d argue that the Brujah and the Toreador epitomising the modern vampiric archetypes strongly suggests that they\u2019re the most visible vampires, which to my mind argues against their being amongst the most numerous (The entire point of The Masquerade is that vampires who get spotted all too often wind up a greasy stain: the less discrete a Clan, the less numerous).<br /> <br /> Although you\u2019re dead right about their embrace rate being quite high (It might well have to be, in order to keep numbers up despite the two Clans having dangerous tendencies towards Vampire Romance).<br /> <br /> <br /> - No arguments over Clan Ventrue: it\u2019s not hard to imagine that a Clan dealing with a Curse that makes blood difficult to come by without careful resource management would be especially meticulous when it comes to keeping the pool of rivals with insider knowledge small, carefully-managed and firmly in your corner.<br /> <br /> <br /> - One wrinkle regarding the Gangrel is that their very mobility makes it easier for them to get away with quasi-legal or otherwise dubious Embraces: being able to pull recruits from unusual places might also make it easier for them to avoid competition with other Clans.<br /> <br /> On the other hand they still have to worry about all the **** Werewolves.<br /> <br /> <br /> - TREMERE: \u201cYou don\u2019t need to know and we have no need to tell you.\u201d<br /> <br /> SABBAT: \u201cBLOOD RITES! BLOOD RITES! B***** RIGHT WE HAVE BLOOD RITES AND EVERY RIGHT TO ENJOY THEM! RAVE ON!\u201d<br /> <br /> IRVINE TELLER: \u201cI don\u2019t know nothing about blood magic.\u201d<br /> <br /> - SHUFFLES RITUAL PARAPHERNALIA OUT OF SIGHT - <br /> <br /> <br /> - Actually, depending on the city Caitiffs might well be the largest single community, if we assume that vampires with a pedigree who don\u2019t yet know as much about that pedigree as they should technically count (and we have multiple examples in the sources of vampires who have no idea who made them, but were brought into the Clan anyway: whilst Caitiffs suffer in the long term, it\u2019s possible that there may be more of a grace period than some might think, especially if a vampire shows strong hints of a specific bloodline).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-17T16:54:06-08:00","id":1557717,"title":"","content":"My Chronicle is set in Brooklyn, NY, which has a little over a hundred Kindred (The Five Boroughs total about 400). I don't design every single one (YIKES), but I have a rough idea of how many are affiliated where.<br /> <br /> The Camarilla is 38 strong - 4 Banu Haqim, 1 Brujah, 2 Lasombra, 5 Malkavians, 6 Nosferatu, 7 Toreador, 4 Tremere, and 9 Ventrue<br /> <br /> The Anarchs have 50 members across a number of gangs, some may be recognizable from <i>New York by Night </i>and <i>The Crimson Gutter</i>: The Night Forum (9 Brujah), The Thrill Kill Gang (4 Caitiff, 2 Gangrel, 2 Thin-Bloods), The Midnighters (2 Lasombra, 1 Malkavian, 1 Toreador), The East Flatbush Dominions (5 Ministry), Number One Heartthrob (2 Lasombra, 1 Malkavian, 1 Nosferatu, 1 Toreador, 2 Tzimisce), The Day Trippers (3 Thin-Bloods), The Carna Island Chantry (4 Tremere), The Night Shift (My PCs--2 Gangrel, 1 Hecata, 1 Nosferatu), and unaffiliated Anarchs (1 Brujah, 2 Caitiff, 1 Ministry, 1 Nosferatu, 1 Ventrue)<br /> <br /> There are 4 Hecata, 3 Banu-Haqim loyal to Ul-Shulgi, and many unaffiliated Kindred (1 Brujah, 8 Caitiff, 1 Malkavian, 3 Nosferatu, 2 Ravnos, 1 Salubri, 1 Thin-Blood, 2 Tzimisce)<br /> <br /> In order, it's 14 Caitiff, 12 Brujah, 12 Nosferatu, 11 Ministry, 10 Ventrue, 9 Toreador, 8 Malkavians, 8 Tremere, 7 Banu Haqim, 6 Lasombra, 6 Thin-Bloods, 5 Hecata, 4 Gangrel, 4 Tzimisce, 2 Ravnos, and 1 Salubri. The demographics of the other boroughs are very different, with the Camarilla clans overrepresented in places like Manhattan and Staten Island, while The Bronx is even more Anarch-coded than Brooklyn. Queens, well, I don't actually have a strong vision for Queens, although I'd imagine it's a patchwork like Brooklyn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6423-higgipedia","name":"higgipedia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6423_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-19T05:27:30-08:00","id":1557756,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557507#post1557507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> - Actually, depending on the city Caitiffs might well be the largest single community, if we assume that vampires with a pedigree who don\u2019t yet know as much about that pedigree as they should technically count (and we have multiple examples in the sources of vampires who have no idea who made them, but were brought into the Clan anyway: whilst Caitiffs suffer in the long term, it\u2019s possible that there may be more of a grace period than some might think, especially if a vampire shows strong hints of a specific bloodline).</div> </div> </div> </div>If your city has an over abundance of Caitiff, it means the Prince is incompetent and not doing their job properly. That's cause for bringing in the Archons to sweep house and put things back in order.<br /> <br /> You don't get special preferential treatment just because you have blood ties to somebody. If your sire didn't wanna stick around, it probably means you were a mistake. Which probably means they won't retaliate, if you suddenly get taken off the census.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-21T19:16:56-08:00","id":1557819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GreatHornedRatkin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557467#post1557467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> While they do travel, it's made pretty clear that doing so is considerably more difficult for them than it is for humans. There's all kinds of precautions and protocols that need to be dealt with, before they can pack up and leave.<br /> <br /> Not least of which involves making sure the vampire government is even going to let you settle down once you step foot in their territory. I seriously doubt the local prince is going to step down off his throne and hand it over, just because you were sitting on top back in your previous domain.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If you think about it, vampires have ALL THE TIME IN THE WORLD to travel; yes; even sans Protean; they have the to walk to the next town or village and find a place to hide from the sun; if they don't find one that night, they can easily walk back to the town or village they started from and go back to that haven. Vampire-travel is slow, and tedious, but it does happen. <br /> <br /> And no prince is going to give up his throne; but not all princes have thrones and some princes are stronger or weaker than others. And if you don't like, the town, or, perhaps you like the prince enough, so, you simply move on to the next town and not even bother. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-29T12:46:14-08:00","id":1557991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557756#post1557756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If your city has an over abundance of Caitiff, it means the Prince is incompetent and not doing their job properly. That's cause for bringing in the Archons to sweep house and put things back in order.<br /> <br /> You don't get special preferential treatment just because you have blood ties to somebody. If your sire didn't wanna stick around, it probably means you were a mistake. Which probably means they won't retaliate, if you suddenly get taken off the census.</div> </div> </div> </div> Your former point stands, the latter is somewhat undermined by VAMPIRE: THE MASQUERADE being nothing if not the game where the right bloodline will quite literally allow you to get away with murder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-29T14:34:05-08:00","id":1557995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557991#post1557991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Your former point stands, the latter is somewhat undermined by VAMPIRE: THE MASQUERADE being nothing if not the game where the right bloodline will quite literally allow you to get away with murder.</div> </div> </div> </div>Princes who abuse the power of the Blood Hunt get taken out in violent coups.<br /> <br /> An uneducated caitiff stepping into the political arena and begins throwing their weight around isn't going to fair much better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-02T09:38:40-08:00","id":1558073,"title":"","content":"Well please do remember that it all depends on how the dice roll: something quite a bit of tie-in fiction and the occasional sourcebook tends to understate (or outright ignore) is that the World of Darkness isn\u2019t a place of predetermined Fate, it\u2019s a game of chance.<br /> <br /> So whilst there\u2019s a good deal of truth in your remarks, sometimes the dice do roll the other way.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-06T09:22:20-08:00","id":1558142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1535037#post1535037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Back in the hay day of WoD they made some GURPS tie-in books, including one for VtM. Some of the best of the GURPS material would include guidance to how to fine tune your game and mechanics for certain styles of play and genre and things like that (kinda like the levers in Scion 2e, now that I think of it). I\u2019ve often commented how much I\u2019d like something like that for WoD, a sort of storytellers took box, but now I wonder if there was anything like that in the old GURPS VtM book.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2446-gryffon\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2446_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2446\" data-vbnamecard=\"2446\">Gryffon</a> which books should I look up for GURPS VtM material?<br /> <br /> To answer the question from the top I am planning a multi clan city as follows. This would be for Kelowna BC circa 2006 - which had 100k people at time, thus wildly violating the standard 100k:1 rule. The small city does have a massive tourist influx, however. A big part of the plot is the area is some kind of relatively psychic magnet for Caitiff and Thinbloods... However that same magnet causes some kind of serious interference for the Werewolves and makes it difficult for them to enter the Okanagan for... reasons.<br /> <br /> So the Shadow Lords invited a powerful Gangrel Elder in to police the thin bloods. Caitiff and thin bloods are divided between waifish/helpless types vs criminal biker gangs that need lots of enforcement, making the Sheriff &amp; Scourge's office quite important and requiring lots of enforcers. The same Magnetic force also seems to make the Masquerade ridiculously easy to follow, as absent camera evidence, mortals in the Okanagan valley seem to forget supernatural encounters by the next day. That got out and caused an influx of Camarilla vampires to come to the city.<ul><li><b>Gangrel:</b> 10</li> <li><b>Brujah:</b> 9</li> <li><b>Toreador:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Ventrue:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Tremere:</b> 7</li> <li><b>Malkavian:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Nosferatu:</b> 2</li> <li><b>Lasombra (antitribu):</b> 3</li> <li><b>Caitiff:</b> 40</li> <li><b>Thin-bloods:</b> 29</li> </ul>The idea is the city is run by a Gangrel Prince with a bunch of her brood supporting her rule. In light of the conversation here, I might make more mono-clan as in some ways might change the tenor and the tone. I think the default idea of the game with vamps from bunches of different clans means competing interests, competing power blocs. The idea for the campaign is that the vampire presence is relatively new - and if the players are not careful, they might actually solve/end the magnetic source for the Caitiff/Thinblood arrival, but also ending the psychic masquerade help as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2783,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","user":"Tytalus","date":". 02-06-2026, 02:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-08T15:42:33-08:00","id":1558194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tytalus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1558142#post1558142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To answer the question from the top I am planning a multi clan city as follows. This would be for Kelowna BC circa 2006 - which had 100k people at time, thus wildly violating the standard 100k:1 rule. The small city does have a massive tourist influx, however. A big part of the plot is the area is some kind of relatively psychic magnet for Caitiff and Thinbloods... However that same magnet causes some kind of serious interference for the Werewolves and makes it difficult for them to enter the Okanagan for... reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>WOW. 1:800 is by far the densest vampire population I've ever seen anyone use. You've got as many Kindred in Kelowna as I have in <b>Brooklyn</b>! The psychic draw is an interesting conceit, that's for sure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6423-higgipedia","name":"higgipedia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6423_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T11:13:52-07:00","id":1562311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1557756#post1557756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If your city has an over abundance of Caitiff, it means the Prince is incompetent and not doing their job properly. That's cause for bringing in the Archons to sweep house and put things back in order.</div> </div> </div> </div>This greatly overestimate how intensely Caitiff are hunted and how much Archons interfere in local affairs.<br /> <br /> Camarilla law establishes both Caitiff and Thin Blood as under normal protection of the law. It isn't usually followed to the letter, but what they suffer is prejudice and unofficial persecution, not a mandate from above to extermination.<br /> <br /> Quite the opposite, if anything a Prince that is openly hunting them on grounds of being what they are alone is way more likely to be seen as disruptive, although still probably left alone.<br /> <br /> And the Camarilla rarely interferes from above on local issues. Its most sacred mandate is the protection of the Traditions, and the Princes or Primogen as legitimate Elders of their Domains<br /> <br /> Princes are also usually quite powerful, brutal and have terrain advantage. Archons and even Justicars don't move against them lightly. And as they deal with such matters however they want, Princes that protect local Caitiff to some extent definitely exist.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tytalus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1558142#post1558142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2446-gryffon\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2446_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2446\" data-vbnamecard=\"2446\">Gryffon</a> which books should I look up for GURPS VtM material?<br /> <br /> To answer the question from the top I am planning a multi clan city as follows. This would be for Kelowna BC circa 2006 - which had 100k people at time, thus wildly violating the standard 100k:1 rule. The small city does have a massive tourist influx, however. A big part of the plot is the area is some kind of relatively psychic magnet for Caitiff and Thinbloods... However that same magnet causes some kind of serious interference for the Werewolves and makes it difficult for them to enter the Okanagan for... reasons.<br /> <br /> So the Shadow Lords invited a powerful Gangrel Elder in to police the thin bloods. Caitiff and thin bloods are divided between waifish/helpless types vs criminal biker gangs that need lots of enforcement, making the Sheriff &amp; Scourge's office quite important and requiring lots of enforcers. The same Magnetic force also seems to make the Masquerade ridiculously easy to follow, as absent camera evidence, mortals in the Okanagan valley seem to forget supernatural encounters by the next day. That got out and caused an influx of Camarilla vampires to come to the city.<ul><li><b>Gangrel:</b> 10</li> <li><b>Brujah:</b> 9</li> <li><b>Toreador:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Ventrue:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Tremere:</b> 7</li> <li><b>Malkavian:</b> 8</li> <li><b>Nosferatu:</b> 2</li> <li><b>Lasombra (antitribu):</b> 3</li> <li><b>Caitiff:</b> 40</li> <li><b>Thin-bloods:</b> 29</li> </ul>The idea is the city is run by a Gangrel Prince with a bunch of her brood supporting her rule. In light of the conversation here, I might make more mono-clan as in some ways might change the tenor and the tone. I think the default idea of the game with vamps from bunches of different clans means competing interests, competing power blocs. The idea for the campaign is that the vampire presence is relatively new - and if the players are not careful, they might actually solve/end the magnetic source for the Caitiff/Thinblood arrival, but also ending the psychic masquerade help as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>For GURPS VtM is literally <i><b>GURPS Vampire: the Masquerade</b></i>, plus a Companion. There's also GURPS Werewolf and GURPS Mage, but only corebooks.<br /> <br /> This city of yours is definitely an interesting concept, but also an ecological nightmare. Not saying you shouldn't do it, rather that you should add the consequences to the fun.<br /> <br /> Setting the Masquerade aside, a vampire need 1 BP a day just to exist, that's 30 a month. 1 BP is also 1 of damage to the victim and depletion of iron. Blood is replenished in less than 24hs, but iron reserves take about a month. There's a reason it's advisable to not donate blood more than two or three times a year.<br /> <br /> If we assume a minimum 3 months cycle to be sure everyone remains healthy and bleeding, a vampire would need to reliably cycle through 90 mortals just for basic needs. Every extra BP uses is another mortal out of the cycle for 90 days.<br /> <br /> Add accidents, causes for those people to lose blood elsewhere, donations, and vampires that take more than 1 BP per vessel (which is common), and this city is under extreme stress if things aren't very tightly regulated.<br /> <br /> Tourism adds a buffer, but be wary of that. The areas that attract them are probably disputed heavily, as it's rare for them to wander literally all around the place. They're also tricky targets, easy to disappear with if you can place a trap, but high profile if you're not cautious. One less boarding the airplane is nothing, a gruesome death of a visitor in a touristic place is serious problems.<br /> <br /> A vampire that can control a few venues where hunting is easier and impose rules can amass quite some influence with it, providing safe feeding in an otherwise severely compromised area. And the Prince has every reason to keep a tight leash on tourist feeding outside such venues to avoid problems that can drive away future visitors.<br /> <br /> Remember: inhabitants just live there and have little choice. Tourists can watch the news and just go somewhere else. This isn't uncommon.<br /> <br /> Kelowna is also relatively safe. It's one of the highest crime rates in Canada, but mostly as property crimes and drug traffic. Domestic violence is somewhat high, but people going missing, murdered or something are things that will call attention.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T14:38:43-07:00","id":1562322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562311#post1562311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the Camarilla rarely interferes from above on local issues. Its most sacred mandate is the protection of the Traditions, and the Princes or Primogen as legitimate Elders of their Domains<br /> <br /> Princes are also usually quite powerful, brutal and have terrain advantage. Archons and even Justicars don't move against them lightly. And as they deal with such matters however they want, Princes that protect local Caitiff to some extent definitely exist.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Princes may be elders of their domain, but the inner circle are the elders of those elders. With more than a few being well over 1,000 years old.<br /> <br /> And, by Camarilla law, the elders have the right to dictate who lives and dies within vampire society.<br /> <br /> An Archon showing up has to be presented as a major deal that threatens the status quo. Because if all they were was a bunch of distant mediators who rarely do anything, then nobody would respect their authority, and their status would become irrelevant.<br /> <br /> It takes more than a shiny crown and fancy title to earn the respect of a society that's pathologically traitorous to each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T18:06:04-07:00","id":1562325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562322#post1562322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Princes may be elders of their domain, but the inner circle are the elders of those elders. With more than a few being well over 1,000 years old.<br /> <br /> And, by Camarilla law, the elders have the right to dictate who lives and dies within vampire society.<br /> <br /> An Archon showing up has to be presented as a major deal that threatens the status quo. Because if all they were was a bunch of distant mediators who rarely do anything, then nobody would respect their authority, and their status would become irrelevant.<br /> <br /> It takes more than a shiny crown and fancy title to earn the respect of a society that's pathologically traitorous to each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd like to remind you that my knowledge comes from the actual books, not YouTube.<br /> <br /> 1. Camarilla law stops at the Prince/Primogen. Above that the Justicars are like the Interpol: their jurisdiction is broad, but relies on local recognition. They can challenge Princes, but they avoid to, because it doesn't always end well for them.<br /> <br /> 2. The Inner Circle is an informal cabal of high level conspirators. They're the most powerful Princes of the Sect, extremely influential, but they have no formal authority over others, nor would be able to keep it if they tried a more direct method.<br /> <br /> 3. Camarilla law recognizes the right of the Elder <u><b>of the Domain</b></u>. This is not anyone or just by seniority. This is the Prince. The whole goal of the Camarilla is to protect the institution of the Prince/Primogen, because that's what let the Inner Circle feel safe in their own Domains. Excess intervention will set a very dangerous precedent, as the Sect only has power as long as the Princes recognize its value.<br /> <br /> 4. Precisely because that's a society of pathological traitors they use manipulation and careful moves instead of open bullying against other powerful members. Weaklings are more or less fair game, but such display of brute force without a very good reason would only make you too many enemies.<br /> <br /> 5. It's precisely because Archons showing up in numbers is a big deal that they shouldn't do that for petty reasons. That would undermine their authority way more than anything else. And in this regard, being distant mediators that rarely do anything is the point. That's what signals that when they do something, this is serious business.<br /> <br /> 6. They're also, you know, occupied with their own lives, power games, turf wars, and the other cities around the world within the Camarilla (and actually they have to worry about Sabbat and others, too).<br /> <br /> 7. Culling Caitiffs is still not Camarilla law. Or killing Thin Bloods. I don't know where you took this bizarre idea that every faction hunts them relentlessly when this being actually unsanctioned superstition, again, is the point.<br /> <br /> No, wait, I do know. At least have an idea. Must watch them myself any day.<br /> <br /> Anyway, Camarilla law protects all Vampires in principle, even if not in practice. The Inner Circle is precisely the group that's most fiercely against hunting them, because they think this will bring way more trouble than benefits. Even V5, that butchered the Camarilla conceptually has most cities accepting them if grudgingly and looking for excuses.<br /> <br /> This has been the law in 1st edition core. In 2nd edition core. In Revised core. In V20 core and in V5 core. Also in most supplements, if not all because authors are sometimes inconsistent. I've read those books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T18:34:14-07:00","id":1562327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562325#post1562325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd like to remind you that my knowledge comes from the actual books, not YouTube.<br /> <br /> 1. Camarilla law stops at the Prince/Primogen. Above that the Justicars are like the Interpol: their jurisdiction is broad, but relies on local recognition. They can challenge Princes, but they avoid to, because it doesn't always end well for them.<br /> <br /> 2. The Inner Circle is an informal cabal of high level conspirators. They're the most powerful Princes of the Sect, extremely influential, but they have no formal authority over others, nor would be able to keep it if they tried a more direct method.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <b>Parliament of Knives</b> seems to suggest otherwise. As the Seneschal and rest of the court is pretty much expected to offer their full cooperation with the Archon when she shows up to restore order. She even demonstrates the authority to assign a new Sensechal, without consulting anybody else beforehand. Also, during the end of the game, the archon decides to hold a public execution of the Anarch leader, who was previously good friends with the Prince and was trying to solve the mystery of his disappearance.<br /> <br /> Even though the Prince is very clearly furious about this decision, he does not directly intervene to stop her; and even thanks you, if you decide to kill the Archon with the executioner's blade.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562325#post1562325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 5. It's precisely because Archons showing up in numbers is a big deal that they shouldn't do that for petty reasons. That would undermine their authority way more than anything else. And in this regard, being distant mediators that rarely do anything is the point. That's what signals that when they do something, this is serious business.<br /> <br /> 6. They're also, you know, occupied with their own lives, power games, turf wars, and the other cities around the world within the Camarilla (and actually they have to worry about Sabbat and others, too).</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say they show up in numbers, just that one shows up in the city.<br /> <br /> Well, the Sabbat isn't (much) of a concern in 5th edition anymore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562325#post1562325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> 7. Culling Caitiffs is still not Camarilla law. Or killing Thin Bloods. I don't know where you took this bizarre idea that every faction hunts them relentlessly when this being actually unsanctioned superstition, again, is the point.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, of course. How silly of me.<br /> <br /> Using a magical cattle iron to permanently brand their flesh for all to see is much better than simply executing them.<br /> <br /> Seriously, how does that not threaten the Masquerade?<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T19:20:27-07:00","id":1562329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562327#post1562327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Parliament of Knives</b> </div> </div> </div> </div>Is an Indie videogame. Licensed, but not canon except for any piece Paradox chooses to use.<br /> <br /> Also:<br /> <br /> 1. Shows a very particular situation, it's not any petty reason for the Archon Adeline to appear.<br /> <br /> 2. Uses V5, where the Camarilla works in a very weird way, and even then the game points out that the Prince is way more powerful than she thinks.<br /> <br /> 3. Politics. The world isn't a matter of punching things to get it your way, and certainly Vampire isn't like that. Adeline gets to murder that guy because she built the circumstances to do so. She has the political upper hand because she has evidence to argue she's just applying the law and helping everyone, and she knows the Prince can't assume publicly that the Anarch is his lover, or he'll be politically screwed. At no point it was a matter of strength among them, the context was the key.<br /> <br /> 4. Even then Adeline is repeatedly shown to not thinking through her actions nearly as much as she should, she's just abusing power, preparing her own bed. In most or every ending she ends up royally screwed. That includes all endings where the Prince is restored to power because, you know, he's actually stronger and she's powerless when he gets the opportunity to kill her with his own hands with impunity. Impunity he has because he IS the Prince.<br /> <br /> Vampire is a political game. Complex contexts and nuance are a big part of it. You think I'm saying that Princes are always above Archons, that's not it. The point is that no one can get away with stupidity. And an Archon going to a city just to complain about Caitiff not being properly spanked is utter stupidity. As would have been for the Prince in PoK to protect the guy he was publicly supposed to kill.<br /> <br /> By the way, Adeline is supposed to be a strong Archon, not average. She's very powerful for her age, despite overextending this power in Ottawa. An average Archon would invoke the authority of their Justicar way more, and still wouldn't feel safe pushing a Prince that far, not without serious backup... Like the backup Adeline had but didn't care to preserve through the game.<br /> <br /> On the magical cattle iron: in Legacy this is not a thing. It's one of the many changes in V5 that get a lot of people annoyed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T20:01:02-07:00","id":1562330,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562329#post1562329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is an Indie videogame. Licensed, but not canon except for any piece Paradox chooses to use.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had assumed that because Swansong, Coteries and (I think?) Nightroad were all considered canon, that Parliament would have fallen in the same category.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562329#post1562329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> On the magical cattle iron: in Legacy this is not a thing. It's one of the many changes in V5 that get a lot of people annoyed.</div> </div> </div> </div>What do you mean Legacy? I thought the branding thing was introduced in V5, rather than prior to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T20:06:36-07:00","id":1562332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562330#post1562330\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I had assumed that because Swansong, Coteries and (I think?) Nightroad were all considered canon, that Parliament would have fallen in the same category.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> What do you mean Legacy? I thought the branding thing was introduced in V5, rather than prior to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those games are partly canon, not completely, for several reasons. And that won't change any of my observations.<br /> <br /> Yes, the branding was introduced in V5, that's what I said. And V5 has a Camarilla that's a total departure of what the Camarilla was, imo halfway as badly retconned as W5. So I do not care about their changes to the law unless the question specifies V5.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T20:36:02-07:00","id":1562333,"title":"","content":"My understanding of this issue is:<br /> <br /> 1) There is ongoing dispute between the Princes and the Inner Circle as to how much authority the Inner Circle has over them. It's like any feudal kingdom where the powerful barons and magnates resent royal authority. The Inner Circle constantly tries to gain more authority, and the Princes constantly try to keep what autonomy they have or regain what they lost.<br /> <br /> 2) Justicars have various duties, and one of them is to investigate. Things like overpopulation in general, disorder, the presence of Caitiff, and so on can are probably within the domain of the Justicar to investigate. What direct actions they can take is another.<br /> <br /> 3) One of the primary powers of the Justicars is to call a Conclave. They can use them to dispose Princes legally, and to do all kinds of chicanery provided they are able to introduce the matter for deliberation and then obtain the necessary votes for the measure (likely doing their best to hide all evidence they are behind it). Conclaves and their rulings are actually quite a big deal, but they are under represented in the setting because we've been given little guidance on how ST should run a conclave in game and that many players wouldn't find such an experience enjoyable. However, conclaves are a way to justify a lot of things in the setting that seem out of the ordinary, but also seem justifiable.<br /> <br /> 4) How Caitiffs are treated is very nebulous in the setting. We have plenty of sourcebooks that present domains as having lots of Caitiff. These are generally domains where the Prince's authority is weak, and the elders are weak or divided. We also have plenty of sourcebooks that present domains seemingly without Caitiff at all. These are generally domains that have a very strong Prince who is able to assert his will over the Kindred of that domain, and the elders are strong or united. To me that suggests &quot;properly run&quot; domains don't tolerate Caitiffs. Those that are inadvertently made simply disappear. However, when authority in a domain is weak, and anarchs are around, the population of Caitiff rises.<br /> <br /> 5) I don't think the Inner Circle, as a rule, is stupid enough to openly state they are against Caitiff and want them destroyed. It's a PR disaster that gains them nothing. However, they clearly don't like them and probably remove any Caitiff troublemakers behind the scenes secretly, and intrigue to support those Princes and elders that hold the view they should be gotten rid of.<br /> <br /> 6) Like a lot of concepts introduced in Revised and later editions, those are perfectly fine if you intend to run a chronicle in the metaplot direction they want. However, lots of players don't want tor run chronicles using the metaplot. In which case, ignore those innovations you don't like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T21:27:41-07:00","id":1562334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562333#post1562333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 5) I don't think the Inner Circle, as a rule, is stupid enough to openly state they are against Caitiff and want them destroyed. It's a PR disaster that gains them nothing. However, they clearly don't like them and probably remove any Caitiff troublemakers behind the scenes secretly, and intrigue to support those Princes and elders that hold the view they should be gotten rid of.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I respectfully disagree. If the books contradict me, then I consider that section of the book to be an overlooked plot hole.<br /> <br /> The general assessment of Caitiff seems to be that they are abandoned shortly after their embrace. Leaving them confused and lacking proper education in the way of things.<br /> <br /> That alone makes them a massive risk to the Masquerade, especially if they're at risk of Frenzying.<br /> <br /> All it takes is one single vampire getting exposed to the world, and the entire security net falls apart.<br /> <br /> The Camarilla can not afford to have a large number of Caitiff roaming around unchecked.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":29009,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","user":"DarkKing","date":". 07-30-2026, 12:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T21:41:22-07:00","id":1562335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562333#post1562333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My understanding of this issue is:</div> </div> </div> </div>1. Originally the Inner Circle was hinted to exist and was a conspiracy, not a formal thing. This partially changed in the sense that they got inconsistent depictions as being a formal thing. But as said, inconsistent. Only Revised almost completely formalize them and then V5 goes all the nine yards.<br /> <br /> That said, this is mostly irrelevant. Politics still mean they're not micromanaging every city and don't have absolute power. Straining things too much will do them no favors. So they're not sending people for petty reasons (Adeline in the game had personal reasons to be there) or to just bluntly impose their personal views.<br /> <br /> 2. Justicars can investigate, but their authority is nebulous. Most avoid making enemies with reason, especially among Princes. Karl Schreck from the Tremere is known as the most willing to step on toes in the line of duty, and as result he was once sent to the Inner Circle packed in a box. The Prince who did this is known and never faced any consequence. Schreck is the single most important Justicar until recent nights, not a mere Archon.<br /> <br /> So they do need recognition, even if it's usually not advisable to just deny them. It's a political game, as always, but not one where the Prince is in a weak position by default. An Archon that abuses office... Just look at what happened to Adeline despite being abnormally powerful for an Archon and arriving at a moment when her target was, indeed, in a weak position.<br /> <br /> 3. Conclaves are a rare thing nonetheless. The Justicars know better than calling one without very good reason. A Prince that tolerates the Caitiff isn't, by itself, reason to send an Archon. Calling a Conclave is just madness.<br /> <br /> 4. Sorry, but there's nothing nebulous about this, it's one of the few things that has been quite consistent in every single edition. The Caitiff are feared by many Elders as signs of Gehenna, but this is formally against the law. What happens is that how this tension resolves or even how deep it runs varies.<br /> <br /> In most Domains there's some balance: no one will bat and eye if a Caitiff disappears, and the Sheriff will frequently ignore crimes against the clanless, or even participate in them, but things can't get out of hand, and out of hand is not a uniform thing. How Princes deal with it is ultimately their problem, and Princes that give some real protection to Caitiff do exist, as do exist Domains where local opinions don't favor being too harsh on them.<br /> <br /> Finally, being a Caitiff isn't a crime, but being an unauthorized Embrace is. Standard procedure is to forfeit the lives of both Sire and Childe. In Bloodlines it is a plot point that LaCroix spares the Fledgling to ease tensions and avoid even more support to Nines.<br /> <br /> Given that Caitiff are more common among the unauthorized Embraces by an abnormally and not understood margin, it's natural that strong Princes will end up with less of them regardless.<br /> <br /> 5. That's explicitly not true for a few reasons.<br /> <br /> While their actual beliefs are uncertain, the Inner Circle established the secularity of the Camarilla and forbade Noddism. One of the few things outside the Traditions that they expect from every Prince is to curb beliefs in Gehenna as much as possible, as they are this as an even worse threat to the status quo than the actual Gehenna (that they really aren't as convinced that will happen as others).<br /> <br /> IIRC the Camarilla does a 180\u00ba in V5, but their stance on Caitiff doesn't change significantly.<br /> <br /> Overall the Elder's superstition against Caitiff isn't seen as disruptive to the Cam, not something to be supported even from the shadows, but Caitiff are also not important enough to be worth too much trouble.<br /> <br /> 6. I don't like, but that's beside the point. With or without metaplot, the notion of an Archon coming to a city only because they think the Prince should be culling the Caitiff is ridiculous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T21:47:52-07:00","id":1562336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534152-clan-demographics-in-your-city?p=1562334#post1562334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I respectfully disagree. If the books contradict me, then I consider that section of the book to be an overlooked plot hole.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's because the books considered things you didn't.<br /> <br /> 1. Caitiff are not always abandoned and uneducated,<br /> <br /> 2. They can be educated, that's fairly simple and easier than hunting them,<br /> <br /> 3. Some Caitiff come from the outside, fleeing other Domains, and already arrive with some common sense.<br /> <br /> 4. This is not about them &quot;roaming unchecked&quot;, they're expected to know and follow the same law as everyone.<br /> <br /> 5. Caitiff are far from the worst offenders of the Masquerade. Quite the opposite, the need to keep a low profile means they're overall the least likely to get in serious breaches.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T22:11:46-07:00","id":1562337,"title":"","content":"I remember how Tremere Justicar Karl Schreck was humiliated by the Ventrue Prince of Berlin. As the Prince he has the authority to decide on how strict the laws of tradition are and him putting a Tremere neonate to final death for not introducing himself in a set period of time is well within his rights.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-30T03:19:01-07:00","id":1562338,"title":"","content":"And Schreck isn't any Justicar. He's the oldest in the job and basically created every procedure, rule and institution for it. Including the Archons.<br /> <br /> His tale is a fine example of what it means to be a Justicar vs a Prince. Schreck has a great amount of clot, undoubtedly. But in the end he is no Prince, and can only push things so far as he can justify his actions. Beyond a threshold, no one will defend him in grounds of authority alone.<br /> <br /> It's pretty much like many auditory or similar institutions in agencies and corporations. They have jurisdiction, not command. Their power is limited precisely by the fact that they are not above those they watch, just given a mandate to certain interventions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}